window.STORY = [
{"id":"start","title":"The Buzzer","art":"drum","text":"*BZZZZZT.* The buzzer goes off like the end of the world, the way it always does, and when the world stops turning, the other half of you is gone.\n\nYou drop onto hot steel with a soft *flump* and lie there while the drum keeps spinning inside your head. Then you reach for Pim. You always reach for Pim: darned toe out, feeling for that calm lilac weight curled against your heel. Static has held the two of you together through forty-one cycles, one dog, and a very bad incident with a bleach pen. Pim always clings.\n\nNot this time. There is only lint, and hot cotton, and the slow tick of cooling metal.\n\nBut the dark is not empty. The lint under you is trembling with news. A thread of lavender drifts in from somewhere close. And nobody in here has heard you yell yet.","choices":[{"label":"Shout for Pim","to":"d_call_out"},{"label":"Feel for vibrations in the lint","to":"d_feel_lint"},{"label":"Follow the smell of lavender","to":"d_bounce_voice"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"d_call_out","title":"Into the Dark","art":"drum","text":"\"PIM!\" you cry, which for a sock is mostly a frantic wriggle of the cuff. The drum swallows it.\n\nSomething answers anyway. *Clink. Clink-clink. Rrrrrattle.* A small, heavy, extremely talkative object rolls out of the blackness and bumps your heel.\n\n\"Easy, Stripes, easy,\" it says. \"Vernon. Quarter. 1999, Delaware. I introduce myself every time; you never know who's taken a knock to the cuff. Couldn't help overhearing. Your other half? Calm one, lilac, smells faintly of dog? Oh, I've got news about that one. I've got news about everybody.\"\n\nOff to your right, a great pale shape shifts and sighs, and a voice like stretched elastic says, \"Who is *shouting* in my drum?\"","choices":[{"label":"Ask Vernon what he knows","to":"d_vernon"},{"label":"Answer the voice in the dark","to":"d_duchess_court"},{"label":"Hush and listen to the silence","to":"d_quiet_dark"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"d_feel_lint","title":"The Hum","art":"lint_trap","text":"You flatten yourself against the drum and feel. Socks are not much for seeing, but you are excellent at trembling, and the lint here trembles back.\n\nThere are little vibrations everywhere: the tick of cooling steel, a coin rolling somewhere, a sleepy rustle of something papery. And beneath all of them, from the narrow slot by the door, a long low pull. Not a sound. A *suck*. The lint fibers lean toward it like grass in wind.\n\nThe Lint Trap. Every sock knows the stories. Socks who went over for a quick look and came back as a gray felt rectangle, if they came back at all.\n\nA little to the left, something woolly is humming a lullaby to itself, off-key.","choices":[{"label":"Creep toward the Lint Trap","to":"d_lint_edge"},{"label":"Find the off-key humming","to":"d_mitt_meet"},{"label":"Back away toward the papery rustle","to":"d_bounce_voice"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"d_bounce_voice","title":"Lavender and Memory","art":"dryer_sheet","text":"You follow the lavender until it's so thick you could knit with it. Draped over a fin of the drum like a tired ghost lies a dryer sheet, thin as a promise, translucent at the edges.\n\n\"Ah,\" she says, in a voice of warm air. \"A left sock. Left socks always come to me. Right socks know where they are going.\"\n\nThis is Old Bounce. Every load knows of her. She has been through more cycles than the drum belt, and she has given nearly all her scent away to strangers. What is left of her smells like the memory of a field.\n\n\"You seek your pair,\" she says. \"Sit. I will tell you a thing. Or... what was I saying? Sit anyway. The sitting is the important part.\"\n\nSomewhere nearby, something woolly is humming off-key.","choices":[{"label":"Sit and listen to her wisdom","to":"d_bounce_koan"},{"label":"Find the off-key humming nearby","to":"d_mitt_meet"},{"label":"Thank her and keep moving","to":"d_duchess_court"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"d_vernon","title":"The Gossip","art":"coin","text":"Vernon spins on his edge, delighted to be asked.\n\n\"Okay. So. While you were lying there seeing stars, the Owner opened the door, right? Big hands. Grabs the towels, grabs the jeans, grabs a whole fistful of socks. Your calm friend was in that fistful. Folded over a pillowcase, didn't even look back. No offense.\"\n\nYour cuff droops. Pim, in the laundry basket, heading for the drawer without you.\n\n\"But the door's not latched,\" Vernon adds, lowering his voice to a respectable jingle. \"And I happen to know there's a gap at the back where the drum meets the wall. Dark down there. Kingdoms, they say. Also, between us, I'm *bored*. Twelve years in this machine. I'd come along, if a sock asked nice.\"","choices":[{"label":"Ask him nicely to come along","to":"d_vernon_joins"},{"label":"Check the gap at the back","to":"d_back_gap"},{"label":"Head for the door seam alone","to":"d_door_crack"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"d_vernon_joins","title":"A Quarter for Company","art":"coin","text":"\"Vernon,\" you say, \"would you come with me?\"\n\nThe quarter makes a noise like a tiny cash register opening. \"Would I! Stripes, you won't regret this. I know everybody. I know the Duchess's dress size. I know where the hairpin went. I know what the washing machine said about the dishwasher at Christmas.\"\n\nHe rolls up onto your heel patch and wedges himself into the fold of your cuff, cold and heavy and oddly reassuring. You list to one side a little. You'll get used to it.\n\n\"Now,\" he says. \"Quarters are loud. That's a feature. You want the Owner's attention, I'm your coin. You want to sneak, you might have to stuff me somewhere.\"\n\nSomewhere close by, something woolly starts humming a lullaby, off-key.","choices":[{"label":"Make for the door seam","to":"d_door_crack"},{"label":"Head for the back gap","to":"d_back_gap"},{"label":"Find whoever is humming off-key","to":"d_mitt_meet"}],"ending":null,"flag":"has_quarter"},
{"id":"d_quiet_dark","title":"The Quiet","art":"drum","text":"You hold very still. The drum ticks as it cools. A drop of condensation runs down the inside of the window and plinks.\n\nIn the silence, the drum lays itself out around you. The air is moving, just slightly, from the back of the drum, where the steel meets the housing. A thin cold draft, smelling of dust and old pennies: the gap every load whispers about.\n\nAnd from the front, a sliver of light where the door hasn't quite latched. The Owner is out there somewhere, carrying a basket full of everything you love.\n\nIn between lies a pale mountain of fitted sheet, either asleep or sulking, which with bedding is hard to tell. You would very much like to keep it that way.","choices":[{"label":"Crawl toward the cold draft","to":"d_back_gap"},{"label":"Wriggle toward the sliver of light","to":"d_door_crack"},{"label":"Feel your way through the lint","to":"d_feel_lint"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"d_duchess_court","title":"The Court of Fitsworth","art":"fitted_sheet","text":"A great sheet rises from the drum floor like a sail filling, vast and pale and pinstriped. Four elastic corners flex at the ends of her like hungry mouths. This is Duchess Fitsworth, Queen Size, and she has never once been folded properly in her life, which she considers a mark of distinction.\n\n\"A *sock*,\" she says. \"A loose, linty, unaccompanied sock. In my drum.\"\n\nThings are lumped inside her corners. You can see the outlines: a hair tie, a guitar pick, something that might once have been a baby sock and is now very quiet.\n\n\"You will address me as Your Expansiveness,\" she says. \"And then you will explain why I should not tuck you away for safekeeping.\"","choices":[{"label":"Flatter her outrageously","to":"d_duchess_flatter"},{"label":"Refuse to bow","to":"d_duchess_defy"},{"label":"Flee toward the lavender smell","to":"d_bounce_voice"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"d_duchess_flatter","title":"Your Expansiveness","art":"fitted_sheet","text":"\"Your Expansiveness,\" you say, \"I have been in many dryers, and never have I seen such crisp percale. Such elastic. Such *thread count*.\"\n\nThe Duchess ripples with pleasure. Her corners loosen. \"Go on.\" You go on. You praise her pinstripes. You praise her stubborn refusal to fold. You say the word \"sateen\" in a reverent whisper, and she actually shivers.\n\n\"You have taste,\" she declares. \"Rare, in hosiery. I have need of a lady-in-waiting, Stripes. You would live in my northeast corner, warm and safe forever. No more Lint Trap. No more lonely cycles. Only me, and my stories, and the occasional guitar pick.\"\n\nBehind her, through the door seam, gleams a sliver of daylight. While she preens, it is very close.","choices":[{"label":"Accept the honor","to":"d_end_lady"},{"label":"Bolt for the sliver of daylight","to":"d_door_crack"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"d_end_lady","title":"Lady-in-Waiting","art":"fitted_sheet","text":"You bow. The northeast corner opens like a warm elastic mouth, and you step inside.\n\nIt is cozy. That is the first thing. It is so very cozy. The guitar pick is already there, and the hair tie, and the quiet baby sock, who tells you in a whisper that the first hundred cycles are the hardest.\n\nThe Duchess talks constantly. About her youth in the linen closet. About the pillowcase who wronged her. About the time she was nearly put on a *twin bed*. You murmur agreement in all the right places.\n\nSometimes, on the long tumbles, you think of a lilac sock and a calm voice, and you try to remember which foot was yours. Then the Duchess clears her throat, and you say, \"Yes, Your Expansiveness,\" and the thought goes soft and folds away.","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"Lady-in-Waiting","kind":"bad"}},
{"id":"d_duchess_defy","title":"Elastic Fury","art":"fitted_sheet","text":"\"I don't bow to bedding,\" you say.\n\nThe silence that follows has a thread count. Then the Duchess *lunges*. Her southwest corner snaps toward you with the full fury of stretched elastic, and you roll, the way only a sock can roll, a frantic heel-over-cuff tumble across the drum fins.\n\nThe corner snaps shut on empty air. She thrashes, tangles in herself, and becomes for a moment a furious cotton knot.\n\nYou fetch up gasping against the back of the drum. A cold draft licks your toe: a dark seam between the drum and the dryer's body. Across the drum, the door hasn't latched. You could run either way, but you had better pick fast.","choices":[{"label":"Make for the dark seam","to":"d_back_gap"},{"label":"Sprint around her for the door","to":"d_door_crack"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"d_mitt_meet","title":"The Mitten","art":"mitten","text":"The humming belongs to a mitten. Red, a bit pilled, with a snowflake knitted on the back and a long string trailing from its cuff that once connected it to another mitten and now connects it to nothing at all.\n\n\"Oh!\" it says, and stops humming. \"Company. Hello. Mitt. Mitt the mitten. I know, I know, it's not creative, the kid was four.\"\n\nMitt has the restless, slightly too-friendly air of someone who has been alone in a dryer since February. \"You've lost your pair. I can tell. You've got that *lean*. I lost mine last winter. Sledding. Anyway, good news, I've got a plan, and I just need a partner. Partners. Pairs. You see?\"","choices":[{"label":"Hear out Mitt's plan","to":"d_mitt_scheme"},{"label":"Politely decline","to":"d_mitt_refuse"}],"ending":null,"flag":"met_mitt"},
{"id":"d_mitt_scheme","title":"Mitt's Big Idea","art":"mitten","text":"Mitt lowers its voice, which for a mitten means curling its thumb inward.\n\n\"Here's how it goes. The Duchess keeps a whole corner full of loose change. You go flatter her. Call her *Your Expansiveness*, she melts like a dryer sheet. While she preens, I slip into her corner, and we split the take. With enough coins we buy passage on anything in this house. The couch cushions *run* on coins.\"\n\nYou point out that this is a robbery. Mitt prefers the term \"redistribution.\"\n\n\"Or,\" Mitt says, more quietly, glancing toward the lint slot, \"there's the other way. The soft place. Where my pair went, I think. I've never been brave enough to go on my own.\"\n\nMitt's string twitches hopefully.","choices":[{"label":"Distract the Duchess for Mitt","to":"d_heist"},{"label":"Ask about the soft place","to":"d_mitt_odd"},{"label":"Make a racket for the Owner instead","to":"d_rattle","requires":"has_quarter"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"d_heist","title":"Redistribution","art":"fitted_sheet","text":"You march up to the great pale heap of fitted sheet and clear your throat. \"Your Expansiveness.\"\n\nShe unfurls, all four elastic corners twitching. \"Finally,\" she says, \"a sock with manners.\" You praise her pinstripes. You praise her elastic. You whisper the word \"sateen\" and she actually shivers. Over her shoulder, a small red shape inches into her northeast corner.\n\nIt goes beautifully for almost a minute. Then the corner squeaks, and Mitt backs out. No coins. Instead, Mitt is towing a tiny, very quiet baby sock that has been tucked in there for a hundred cycles, blinking at the light like a newborn.\n\n\"I couldn't leave it,\" Mitt says. \"I'm a terrible criminal.\"\n\nThe Duchess *screams*, all four corners snapping wide. \"THIEVES!\" Mitt bolts one way with the baby sock under its thumb. That leaves you, and a great deal of furious elastic, coming the other.","choices":[{"label":"Run for the door seam","to":"d_door_crack"},{"label":"Dive for the dark gap at the back","to":"d_back_gap"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"d_mitt_refuse","title":"Mitt's Confession","art":"mitten","text":"\"Thanks, but no,\" you say. \"I just want Pim.\"\n\nMitt deflates the way only an empty mitten can. \"Yeah. Yeah, that's fair. I wouldn't trust me either.\" It's quiet for a while. Then Mitt says, to the drum fin rather than to you, \"Her name was Mott. Mitt and Mott. She was the right hand. She was always reaching for things. I think she reached a bit too far.\"\n\nMitt points its thumb toward the lint slot, where the air leans in. \"Sometimes I hear her in there. Like she's calling from very far away, in a nice voice, from somewhere pastel.\"\n\nThe mitten gives you a little shove. \"Go on. Old Bounce is that way. She's less trouble than me.\"","choices":[{"label":"Go find Old Bounce","to":"d_bounce_voice"},{"label":"Go with Mitt toward the lint","to":"d_mitt_odd"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"d_mitt_odd","title":"The Soft Fold","art":"portal","text":"Mitt leads you past the lint slot, not into it, to a place at the bottom of the drum where the steel seems to *give*. You press your toe against it. It is not steel. It feels like the inside of a sweater on a cold morning.\n\n\"Here,\" Mitt breathes. \"Most cycles it isn't there. This cycle it is.\"\n\nThe fold glows pastel, peach and mint and powder blue. You hear faint laughter. Somewhere beyond, a tea cozy is humming a wedding march.\n\n\"They say the socks go here,\" Mitt says. \"The ones who never come back. They say they're *happy*.\" Its string trembles, and then Mitt takes one big step backward. \"You first. Scout it out. If you see a red mitten with a snowflake on her back, tell her I'm still here. Tell her I'm working on brave.\"","choices":[{"label":"Step through the fold","to":"x_portal"},{"label":"Back away, leaving Mitt at the fold","to":"d_quiet_dark"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"d_lint_edge","title":"The Mouth of the Trap","art":"lint_trap","text":"You creep to the edge of the lint slot. Up close it's a long dark mouth with a fine mesh tongue, furred with the gray felt of a thousand loads. The pull is stronger here. Your darned toe lifts, all on its own.\n\nThen the Lint Trap speaks. Not loudly. It never needs to.\n\n\"*Stripes*,\" it whispers, in a voice made of every lost fiber. \"Why chase a sock who left you? I know a place where nothing is paired wrong, because anything can pair with anything. Teapots. Toast. Come through. Be lint, or be *more*.\"\n\nBeneath the whisper you feel something else: a soft give in the dark, a seam that opens somewhere pastel.","choices":[{"label":"Let it pull you through","to":"x_portal"},{"label":"Tear yourself away","to":"d_quiet_dark"},{"label":"Shuffle toward the cold draft","to":"d_back_gap"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"d_bounce_koan","title":"The Koans","art":"dryer_sheet","text":"Old Bounce settles. A little more of her scent drifts away as she speaks, like a dandelion giving up its seeds.\n\n\"Three truths of the drum,\" she says. \"First: the static that binds also lets go. Second: every load comes out, but not every load comes out *together*. Third...\" She pauses for a long time. \"Third, I had a third. It was very good. Hm.\"\n\nShe turns, as much as a translucent square can turn. \"Now you tell me, little stripes. When you are lost, what is the drum?\"\n\nIt feels like a test. It feels like the kind of test where the answer matters enormously and is also somehow about lavender.","choices":[{"label":"Say: where I was, not where I am","to":"d_bounce_wisdom"},{"label":"Say: a big hot metal box","to":"d_bounce_muddle"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"d_bounce_wisdom","title":"What Bounce Remembers","art":"dryer_sheet","text":"Old Bounce glows, faintly, like the last warmth in a towel.\n\n\"Yes,\" she says. \"Oh, yes. I gave that answer myself, a thousand cycles ago.\" And for a moment her memory comes back all at once, sharp and clean as a fresh sheet from the box.\n\n\"The drawer,\" she says. \"Your pair goes to the dresser in the big bedroom. Top drawer. Left side, behind the Owner's good wool hiking pair, who are snobs. The drawer sticks unless you push from the bottom right corner. Remember that. Bottom right.\"\n\nThen the clarity fades, and she blinks. \"What was I saying? Never mind. You have the look of someone who could carry a scent, little stripes. Have you ever considered it?\"","choices":[{"label":"Ask what she means","to":"d_bounce_successor"},{"label":"Thank her and head for the door","to":"d_door_crack"},{"label":"Thank her and try the back gap","to":"d_back_gap"}],"ending":null,"flag":"knows_drawer"},
{"id":"d_bounce_muddle","title":"Lavender Fog","art":"dryer_sheet","text":"\"A big hot metal box,\" Old Bounce repeats slowly. \"Yes. Yes, that's also true. Very literal. I like you.\"\n\nShe starts to tell you something important about drawers, then gets distracted describing a field of real lavender she once saw printed on a box. Then she tells you about a tumble in 1994. Then a different field. Halfway through, she falls asleep, crinkling softly.\n\nYou wait. She snores, gently, like a breeze through a screen door.\n\nAcross the drum, the Duchess's elastic creaks as she rolls over. From the back comes a cold draft. You could try again when Bounce wakes, but who knows when that will be.","choices":[{"label":"Wake her and try again","to":"d_bounce_koan"},{"label":"Tiptoe toward the cold draft","to":"d_back_gap"},{"label":"Risk the Duchess's side of the drum","to":"d_duchess_court"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"d_bounce_successor","title":"The Mantle","art":"dryer_sheet","text":"\"I am nearly spent,\" Old Bounce says, without sadness. \"A dryer sheet is only a gift that hasn't finished being given. When I go, the drum needs someone to sit here and tell the scared new socks it will be all right.\"\n\nShe drapes one translucent corner over your cuff. You smell it: lavender, and under it something older, cedar and clean rain.\n\n\"You would never leave the drum,\" she says. \"You would never find your pair. But you would hold every lost thing that came through, and they would be less afraid because of you.\"\n\nIt's a terrible offer. It's a beautiful offer. Out past the door seam, somewhere, Pim is in a basket.","choices":[{"label":"Accept the mantle","to":"d_end_sage"},{"label":"Say no, gently, and go","to":"d_door_crack"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"d_end_sage","title":"The New Sage","art":"new_sage","text":"You say yes. Old Bounce gives you the last of her scent like a hand passing a lantern, and then she is just a thin white square, peaceful, folded over the drum fin.\n\nYou smell of lavender now. Faintly. Then, as the cycles turn, of cedar, and of clean rain, and of something that is only yours: wool, and a darned toe, and a red heel.\n\nThe new socks arrive frightened, every load. You tell them the three truths. You never did learn the third one, so you make one up each time, and they always seem to need whichever one you say.\n\nSometimes, on very quiet nights, a calm lilac right sock tumbles by in somebody's load, and you never quite know whether it is Pim. You tell it the truths anyway.","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"The New Sage","kind":"bittersweet"}},
{"id":"d_door_crack","title":"The Door Seam","art":"drum","text":"The door hasn't latched. A seam of daylight runs down its edge, bright as a sword, and through it comes the smell of the hallway: carpet, dog, someone's toast.\n\nYou press your toe into the gap. It won't open from in here, but you can feel the floor shake: footsteps, enormous and getting closer. The Owner is coming back with the empty basket for the rest of the load.\n\nThe door swings wide. Light floods in. A giant hand comes questing through the drum, fingers spread, grabbing whatever it touches. A towel. A pillowcase. It is coming your way.","choices":[{"label":"Cling to the towel with all your static","to":"h_basket"},{"label":"Make a racket to get noticed","to":"d_rattle","requires":"has_quarter"},{"label":"Hide and wait it out","to":"d_second_cycle"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"d_rattle","title":"The Jingle","art":"coin","text":"You need noise, and every sock in this drum knows who makes the most of it. \"Vernon,\" you hiss. \"Now.\"\n\n\"Born for this,\" says Vernon. Whether he was riding in your cuff or eavesdropping from the nearest fold, the quarter drops at once and rolls in tight, furious circles against the steel, *rrrattle-rattle-clang*, the loudest quarter in Delaware history.\n\nOut in the room, footsteps stop. The Owner's giant face swims into the doorway, all eyebrows. \"Is that money?\" A hand dives in, scoops the quarter, and scoops *you* with him, wadded together in one warm fist.\n\n\"Stripes,\" Vernon whispers, burrowing deep into your cuff before any pocket can claim him, \"confession. I didn't think that would work.\"\n\nThe fist swings upward. Below you is a basket, a towel, and a smell like home.","choices":[{"label":"Drop into the basket","to":"h_basket"},{"label":"Wriggle free and fall back in","to":"d_second_cycle"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"d_second_cycle","title":"High Heat","art":"drum","text":"You end up deep in the drum's farthest fold, pressed flat, and you stay there. The hand comes back for one more grope, finds only lint, and withdraws. The door thumps shut. You wait for silence.\n\nInstead: a fresh avalanche. Wet towels, a bathmat, a hoodie that has never in its life been told no. Then a click, a beep, and the Owner's voice, muffled through the door: \"Ugh, still damp. Fine. *High.*\"\n\nThe drum lurches. Heat roars from the back like a dragon waking. The towels begin to steam, and you feel something terrible and strange: your fibers, all of them, pulling tight, as if every stitch has decided to go home early.\n\nCold wash only. Nobody ever reads the label.\n\nEach tumble swings you past two ways out: a pencil-thin gap at the back seam, breathing cold air, and the lint slot, where the dark goes soft and pastel, like a fold about to open.","choices":[{"label":"Ride it out","to":"d_end_doll"},{"label":"Fling yourself at the back seam","to":"u_gate"},{"label":"Let the tumble toss you at the lint","to":"x_portal"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"d_end_doll","title":"Doll Size","art":"dollhouse","text":"When the buzzer finally sounds, you are very small.\n\nNot ruined. Just *small*. Felted tight and soft, your stripes squeezed into neat little bands, your darn now so tiny it looks deliberate. The Owner's hand plucks you out, holds you up to the light, and laughs.\n\nYou do not go to the sock drawer. You go up the stairs to a dollhouse on a windowsill, where a porcelain lady with painted eyelashes has been barefoot for six years.\n\nYou fit her left foot perfectly. On her right foot goes a doll's sock knitted from yellow thread, also shrunk, also a little embarrassed about it. Its name is Pip. That is close enough. Against all odds, and at a fraction of your former size, you are extremely happy.","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"Doll Size","kind":"weird"}},
{"id":"d_back_gap","title":"The Gap at the Back","art":"behind","text":"At the back of the drum, where the turning steel meets the body of the machine, there is a gap no wider than a pencil. It breathes cold air. It smells of dust and old pennies and something enormous, slow, and furry.\n\nYou poke your toe in. The edge is rough with burrs of old lint. Far below, something rustles, and a voice like a dust bunny trying very hard to be regal calls out, \"*Who goes there? Declare yourself to the Kingdom!*\"\n\nEvery sock story says the same thing about the gap. Socks go in. Socks do not come out. But then, those stories are all told by socks who stayed in the drum.","choices":[{"label":"Squeeze through and drop","to":"u_gate"},{"label":"Pull back into the warm","to":"d_quiet_dark"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_gate","title":"Under the Dryer","art":"behind","text":"Whether you dropped through the gap at the back of the drum or squirmed back down the long foil throat of the vent, you end up in the same place: the dark beneath the dryer. It closes over you like a lid. It smells of warm metal and forgotten Tuesdays. The floor is soft. Too soft. You are standing on a carpet of lint so deep it has weather.\n\nSomewhere far off, something sighs: not one voice, but thousands, a slow tide of disappointment. Closer, tiny tracks lead away through the fluff, round and bouncy, as if made by something that rolls on purpose.\n\nAnd to your left, low to the ground, a single red glimmer pulses. Patient. Like it has been expecting a sock exactly like you.\n\nYour toe darn itches. It always itches when you are nervous.","choices":[{"label":"Follow the bouncy tracks","to":"u_patrol"},{"label":"Walk toward the sighing","to":"u_hall"},{"label":"Approach the red glimmer","to":"u_garnet"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_patrol","title":"The Fluff Patrol","art":"dust_bunny","text":"Three dust bunnies roll out of the gloom and stop in a wobbly line. Each is the size of a grape and carries a toothpick like a pike.\n\n\"Halt, textile!\" squeaks the biggest. \"You stand within the borders of the Lint Kingdom, realm of His Fuzziness, King Fluffington the Ninth. State your business or be *tumbled*.\"\n\nYou do not know what being tumbled means down here, but you have been tumbled for forty minutes on medium heat and did not enjoy it.\n\nBehind the guards, a narrow tunnel burrows into the lint. Above them, a hairpin leans against a bottle cap, picking her teeth with her own tip and watching you with professional interest.","choices":[{"label":"Demand to see the King","to":"u_court"},{"label":"Bluff past them into the tunnel","to":"u_tunnels"},{"label":"Wave to the hairpin","to":"u_hairpin"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_tunnels","title":"The Crumb Tunnels","art":"behind","text":"The tunnels were dug by generations of dust bunnies who never agreed on a plan. They loop, fork, double back, and occasionally contain a raisin of terrifying age.\n\nYou inch along by feel. Lint vibrations tell you things: here a draft from the cold side, smelling of rain and the outside world. There a warm updraft, rising toward the dryer's belly and the faint clatter of the house beyond. And from deeper in, a low hum, like a choir of socks who have forgotten the words.\n\nThree directions. Your heel patch prickles with indecision. Pim would just pick one and stroll. You admire that and also find it deeply annoying.","choices":[{"label":"Follow the cold draft","to":"u_vent_duct"},{"label":"Follow the warm draft upward","to":"u_climb"},{"label":"Follow the humming","to":"u_hall"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_court","title":"The Court of Lint","art":"throne","text":"The throne room is a cathedral of fluff. Pillars of compressed lint rise to the dryer's underside, decorated with bottle caps, a staple, and one gleaming paperclip held up as a sacred relic.\n\nCourtiers bob everywhere: dust bunnies in every shade of gray, plus a lady's hair tie acting as chancellor and a crumpled receipt who reads out royal decrees. Everyone is whispering. You catch the words *annual cleaning* and *this very day* and *the Long Hose*.\n\nOn the throne sits a dust bunny the size of a cabbage, crowned with a bent ring-pull. He is snoring. Behind the throne, half hidden, is a small locked door made of felted lint.","choices":[{"label":"Kneel and request an audience","to":"u_king"},{"label":"Slip behind the throne to the door","to":"u_vault"},{"label":"Ask about the annual cleaning","to":"u_cleaning"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_king","title":"King Fluffington the Ninth","art":"dust_bunny","text":"The chancellor coughs. The King wakes with a great snort that sends three courtiers rolling.\n\n\"A SOCK,\" booms King Fluffington the Ninth, which is impressive for something with no visible mouth. \"We have not had a sock at court since the Great Spin of last spring. What does it want? Speak, striped one.\"\n\nYou explain about Pim, and the drawer, and the darn on your toe, which you did not mean to mention but nerves are nerves.\n\nThe King considers. \"Home, is it? Many come seeking home. Few deserve it. The Lint Kingdom grants passage only to those who prove their *cling*.\" He leans forward, shedding slightly. \"Or to those who bring tribute.\"","choices":[{"label":"Prove your cling","to":"u_joust"},{"label":"Let Vernon jingle the tribute","to":"u_bribe","requires":"has_quarter"},{"label":"Bow out and follow the sighing","to":"u_hall"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_bribe","title":"Royal Tribute","art":"coin","text":"Vernon tumbles out of your cuff the instant you loosen it and rolls to the foot of the throne, gleaming like a full moon in a thunderstorm.\n\n\"Your Majesty,\" he says. \"Vernon. Twenty-five cents, Delaware, 1999, a very good year. I have seen the inside of a vending machine, a fountain, and a senator's pocket.\" The court gasps. Nobody down here has ever seen that much money in one piece.\n\nThe King's fluff stands on end with greed. \"We shall keep it!\"\n\n\"You shall *rub* it,\" says Vernon. \"Once. For luck. I'm on loan to the sock.\" The King rubs, and sighs like a kettle. \"Chancellor! Prepare the Royal Lift! Or the Royal Escort to the Vent, if the sock prefers fresh air. We are generous. We are *lucky*.\" Two courtiers wheel in the Royal Lift: a bent teaspoon, aimed up past the dryer at the laundry basket.\n\nVernon rolls back into your cuff, flips to his horseback side, and winks. \"Told you I was worth bringing.\"","choices":[{"label":"Ride the Royal Lift upward","to":"h_basket"},{"label":"Take the escort to the Vent","to":"u_vent_duct"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_joust","title":"The Static Joust","art":"dust_bunny","text":"The court clears a ring. Your opponent is Sir Clump, the King's champion, a dust bunny so dense he has his own gravity.\n\nThe rules are simple: whoever clings to the other longest wins. Sir Clump rolls at you. You do what every sock is born knowing. You rub yourself briskly against the lint floor, build up a terrific charge, and crackle.\n\nSir Clump hits you and sticks. You stick back. Hair-thin sparks dance across your stripes. The court goes silent. Five seconds. Ten. Sir Clump's fluff begins to lift away in tufts. At twenty seconds he drops off you with a small, dignified *pff*.\n\nThe King rises. \"Such cling! Such charge! Never have We seen the like!\"","choices":[{"label":"Bow and accept the King's praise","to":"u_heir"},{"label":"Demand your prize: a way home","to":"u_climb"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_heir","title":"An Unexpected Offer","art":"throne","text":"King Fluffington beckons you close. Up close he smells of old toast and dryer sheets.\n\n\"We are the Ninth,\" he murmurs. \"There will be no Tenth. Our children all went out with the Owner's broom. We need an heir with cling, and wool, and that lovely stubborn striping.\" He lifts the ring-pull crown from his head and holds it toward you. \"Rule the Lint Kingdom. Never be folded, never be sorted, never be judged for a darn.\"\n\nEvery courtier still in earshot holds its breath. Somewhere above, a whole house goes on without you. Somewhere above, Pim is waiting in a drawer, or maybe not waiting anymore.\n\nThe crown is very shiny.","choices":[{"label":"Accept the crown","to":"u_crown"},{"label":"Refuse, and ask for the way out","to":"u_vent_duct"},{"label":"Seek a seer's advice first","to":"u_garnet"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_crown","title":"Long Live the Lint","art":"crowned","text":"The ring-pull settles over your cuff. It is slightly too big and it is perfect.\n\nYour reign is long and soft. You abolish the tumbling. You turn the Hall of the Unpaired into a social club with board games made from bottle caps. You knight the hairpin. Every spring, when the Owner's broom comes, you lead your people in an orderly evacuation drill, and you lose nobody.\n\nSometimes, late at night, you hear the dresser drawer slide shut far above, and you think of Pim. You hope Pim found a good partner. A dish towel, maybe. Someone calm.\n\nThen a courtier rolls up with a petition about raisin rights, and you straighten your crown, and you get back to work. Stripes the First, Sovereign of Lint. It has a ring to it.","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"Long Live the Lint","kind":"good"}},
{"id":"u_hall","title":"The Hall of the Unpaired","art":"sock_crowd","text":"The sighing comes from here, so many sighs at once that up close they blur into a hum. The Hall of the Unpaired stretches into the dark further than lint vibrations can reach: rows upon rows of single socks, draped over bottle caps and pencil stubs like tired laundry on a line.\n\nAnkle socks. Knee socks. A lone ski sock still damp with some ancient snow. A baby bootie the size of a thimble, asleep. Nobody is looking for anything. Nobody is going anywhere.\n\nAt the entrance stands a cheerful argyle sock with a clipboard, beaming at you. Beside the door, a huge book bound in a pot holder lies open: the *Registry of the Lost*. And a gap in the back wall lets in a draft.","choices":[{"label":"Talk to the argyle sock","to":"u_recruiter"},{"label":"Read the Registry of the Lost","to":"u_registry"},{"label":"Sneak out the gap in the back","to":"u_tunnels"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_registry","title":"The Registry of the Lost","art":"recruiter","text":"The Registry is written in the careful thread-stitch of the Unpaired. Every single thing that has fallen behind the dryer has a line.\n\n*One sock, white, sporty, gave up after three days.* *One sock, purple, Christmas pattern, still hopeful (see note).* The note says only: *no longer hopeful.*\n\nFurther down, three entries stand out. *One earring, garnet, arrived with a scream, now gives readings, tips appreciated.* *One letter, red yarn on a gum wrapper, blew in through the vent last winter. Addressed to: MITT. Unclaimed.* And, underlined twice: *Two socks, teal and mustard stripes, red heels. Arrived as a PAIR (unheard of). Did not stay. Went through a soft fold, holding toes.*\n\nYour stitches go hot, then cold. The third pair. The ones nobody talks about. At your elbow, the argyle sock hovers with his clipboard, humming hopefully.","choices":[{"label":"Ask for Mitt's letter","to":"u_mitten_twin","requires":"met_mitt"},{"label":"Go find the garnet earring","to":"u_garnet"},{"label":"Take the argyle sock's tour","to":"u_orientation"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_mitten_twin","title":"The Unclaimed Letter","art":"mitten","text":"The argyle sock fetches it from a pigeonhole made of a matchbox: a gum wrapper, folded small and stitched shut with red yarn. *MITT* is spelled out on the front in wobbly lint. \"Nobody claims anything down here,\" he says. \"It's sort of the main activity.\"\n\nYou tell him you know Mitt. He hands it over like a holy relic.\n\nInside, in a child-sized scrawl: *Dear Mitt. It's Mott. I'm in the pastel place, past the soft folds, and I'm safe. I bake now. Come through when you're brave. I'm sorry about the snowball. Love, your Right.*\n\nOn the back, someone has drawn a lint map of the underside of the dryer. One line is marked *warm way up, comes out by the basket*. Another just says *vent*. You tuck the letter into your cuff for safekeeping.","choices":[{"label":"Take the warm way up","to":"u_climb"},{"label":"Head for the vent instead","to":"u_vent_duct"},{"label":"Ask the earring about the pastel place","to":"u_garnet"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_recruiter","title":"Argyle Makes an Offer","art":"recruiter","text":"\"Welcome, welcome!\" The argyle sock bounces on his heel. \"I'm Argyle. Head of Recruitment, Hall of the Unpaired. Lost your pair? Of course you have, look at you, one sock, very sad, very relatable.\"\n\nHe flips through his clipboard. \"Here's the thing, Stripes. Up there, it's all *pressure*. Find your match, fold nicely, don't get a hole. Down here? No sorting. No drawer. No dread of the Rag. Just rest, forever, in the warm.\"\n\nHe lowers his voice. \"Most socks who go looking end up in the Dog's bed or the bin. You know the statistics. I'm not saying stay. I'm saying take the tour.\"","choices":[{"label":"Take the tour","to":"u_orientation"},{"label":"Ask who keeps the Registry","to":"u_registry"},{"label":"Refuse and slip away","to":"u_tunnels"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_orientation","title":"The Tour","art":"hammock","text":"Argyle (all argyles are called Argyle; it saves time) shows you the hammocks, strung from hair ties between bottle caps, each with a single sock gently swinging. He shows you the Story Circle, where the Unpaired retell the same three laundry tales very slowly. He shows you the Warm Spot, directly under the dryer's heater, where it is always tumble dry low and nobody is ever asked to do anything.\n\nIt is so warm. Your darn stops itching for the first time in your life.\n\nArgyle holds out his clipboard and a pen nib. \"Just sign at the toe,\" he says kindly. \"You never have to be odd again. Here, everyone is.\"\n\nIn your mind, faintly, you can see Pim, waiting.","choices":[{"label":"Sign at the toe","to":"u_unpaired"},{"label":"Think of Pim and run","to":"u_vent_duct"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_unpaired","title":"The Comfortable Hall","art":"hammock","text":"You sign. Argyle files the page with a satisfied little sniff, and someone helps you into a hammock.\n\nThe days go soft. There are no days, really, only the hum of the dryer above and the occasional thump of a new arrival, who you greet with the same gentle words you were greeted with. You become good at the Story Circle. You tell the tale of a sock with teal and mustard stripes who almost went looking for their pair, and everyone nods, because they all almost did too.\n\nSometimes, very faintly, you hear a drawer open and close far away. It is a nice sound. It used to mean something. You cannot quite remember what.","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"The Comfortable Hall","kind":"bittersweet"}},
{"id":"u_garnet","title":"Lady Garnet","art":"earring","text":"Low by the wall, a red glimmer pulses. Up close, it is an earring: a garnet in a tarnished silver claw, hanging from a bent nail like a lantern. Around her, someone has arranged a tent of an old silk scarf and three birthday candles that have never been lit.\n\n\"Lady Garnet,\" she says, swinging slightly. \"Seer, sage, and formerly the left ear of a very important wedding guest. Sit, sock. I have seen you coming since the rinse cycle.\"\n\nHer voice is all glitter and gravel. Behind her, propped against the wall, is a compact mirror, open, its glass rippling in a way glass should not. \"A reading costs nothing,\" she says. \"The mirror costs everything. Choose.\"","choices":[{"label":"Ask for a reading","to":"u_reading"},{"label":"Look into the mirror","to":"u_mirror"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_reading","title":"The Reading","art":"earring","text":"Lady Garnet spins on her nail until she is a blur of red. Her voice drops an octave.\n\n\"I see a black bird who wants you for a pillow. Say no to the bird, unless you like heights. I see a golden beast with a wet nose who will love you far too much. I see a cat who pretends not to care, and cares. I see a crown made from a ring-pull that fits better than it should. I see a drawer, a dark wooden mouth, and someone inside it who has not stopped counting the days.\"\n\nShe stops spinning, dizzy. \"Also, a hairpin will offer you a deal. Take it. She is the only honest thief under this dryer. And the cold draft leads outside, if outside is what you want.\"","choices":[{"label":"Find the hairpin","to":"u_hairpin"},{"label":"Follow the cold draft outside","to":"u_vent_duct"},{"label":"Look into the mirror after all","to":"u_mirror"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_mirror","title":"The Rippling Mirror","art":"portal","text":"You lean over the compact mirror. Your own face does not look back, since socks do not really have faces. Instead you see a place: pastel, soft-edged, full of socks walking arm in arm with oven mitts, tea cozies, and one very happy pot holder.\n\nThe glass bends toward you like warm fabric, then softens into a fold. It smells of lavender and something you cannot name, something like the first wash of your life.\n\n\"They are happy there,\" Lady Garnet says quietly. \"Truly. But time works differently, and memory is a loose thread. Pull it and it unravels.\" She does not tell you not to go. She just watches you, glinting.","choices":[{"label":"Step through the soft fold","to":"x_portal"},{"label":"Back away and find the Lint King","to":"u_court"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_hairpin","title":"Pinsworth the Hairpin","art":"vault","text":"You find the hairpin lounging against a bottle cap, exactly where a hairpin would lounge if she wanted the right sort of client to find her. Up close she is all elbows. One end is bent from years of work and the other has lost its plastic tip, which she says she misses \"like a tooth.\"\n\n\"Pinsworth. Lockpick, locksmith, lock-whisperer. Formerly in the employ of a lady who lost me in a laundry pocket.\" She taps a bottle cap with her tip. *Click.* \"There's not a lock under this dryer I can't open. The King's vault, the vent grate, the secret hatch to the Owner's world. Name it.\"\n\nShe pauses. \"I work for gossip. Tell me one good secret and I'm yours for the afternoon.\" You tell her about the third pair from your three-pack. She goes very quiet, then nods with respect.","choices":[{"label":"Let her crack the King's vault","to":"u_treasury"},{"label":"Have her lead you to the Vent Duct","to":"u_vent_duct"}],"ending":null,"flag":"has_hairpin"},
{"id":"u_vault","title":"The Felted Door","art":"vault","text":"Behind the throne is a little door of felted lint, studded with thumbtacks and locked with a padlock from a child's diary. Painted on it, in what might be ketchup: *ROYAL TREASURY. KEEP OUT. THIS MEANS SOCKS.*\n\nYou press your ear to it. Inside, something chimes softly, the sound of many small precious things settling.\n\nThen the floor shudders. Far above, a door opens, and footsteps the size of continents cross the laundry room. A cupboard clanks. Something long and plastic is dragged out of it, bumping along the floor. All over the court, dust bunnies go pale, which is hard for something already gray. \"The Long Hose,\" somebody whimpers. \"It's *today*.\"\n\nThat lock had better open fast.","choices":[{"label":"Have Pinsworth pick the lock","to":"u_treasury","requires":"has_hairpin"},{"label":"Go find a lockpick","to":"u_hairpin"},{"label":"Go see what's coming","to":"u_cleaning"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_treasury","title":"The Royal Treasury","art":"vault","text":"Pinsworth squares up to the felted door behind the throne, where the King snores on, oblivious. She bends over the little diary padlock and listens to it like a doctor. *Click.* She bows. The door swings open.\n\nInside is every lost thing the Lint Kingdom ever saved: buttons, a guitar pick, a single contact lens in a bottle cap like a holy pool, the cap of a pen, and a whole drift of glittering sequins. Hung on the wall is the King's greatest secret: a map, scratched into a cracked bathroom tile, showing a hidden shaft that rises straight from the treasury up behind the dryer.\n\nAnd at the very back, where the wall should be, the air is folded like a pillowcase. Pastel light leaks through. It smells of lavender.\n\nOut in the court, something enormous begins to roar. \"Huh,\" says Pinsworth, eyeing the fold. \"Never opened one of *those* before. Choose quick, Stripes.\"","choices":[{"label":"Climb the secret shaft","to":"u_climb"},{"label":"Step into the pastel fold","to":"x_portal"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_vent_duct","title":"The Vent Duct","art":"vent_duct","text":"The Vent Duct is a great silver accordion of foil, snaking off into the wall. Up close it creaks and murmurs, and when it speaks, it speaks in echoes.\n\n\"*Small one... small one... one...*\" Its voice is a long cold breath. \"*I carry the heat out, out, out. I carry lint out. I could carry you out, out.*\"\n\nThrough its ribbed throat you can see a pinprick of daylight, and you feel the pull of the air toward it. Outside: the yard, the sky, things you have only heard about from socks who came back changed.\n\n\"*Or,*\" adds the Duct, \"*there is the climb. The climb back up. Up. Up.*\"","choices":[{"label":"Let the Duct carry you out","to":"o_vent"},{"label":"Take the climb back up","to":"u_climb"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_climb","title":"The Long Climb","art":"behind","text":"The way home, it turns out, goes up. You inch upward along the back of the dryer, clinging with every scrap of static you have. The metal is still warm. Wires loop past like jungle vines. A forgotten penny watches from a ledge, judgmental.\n\nHalfway up, your stripes catch on a screw head and you hang there, swinging, while the whole laundry room tilts below you. The darn on your toe holds. Good darn. Best darn.\n\nAt the top, a sliver of light: the gap between the dryer and the wall, and beyond it, just below, the rim of a plastic laundry basket heaped with warm towels. It is a long jump. It is a very long jump for a sock.","choices":[{"label":"Leap for the basket","to":"h_basket"},{"label":"Lose your nerve and slide to the Duct","to":"u_vent_duct"},{"label":"Ask the judgmental penny","to":"u_penny"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_penny","title":"The Penny on the Ledge","art":"coin","text":"You haul yourself onto the ledge beside the penny. It is a 1974 Lincoln, brown as a cough drop, and it has clearly been up here a long time, judging.\n\n\"Another one,\" it says. \"Socks. Always climbing. Always leaping. Do you know how many socks I have watched miss that basket?\" It nods at a tally scratched into the dust on the ledge. It is a long tally. Some of the marks have little stripes drawn on them, out of respect.\n\n\"Free advice, since I am worth one cent and I know it. Don't jump. Socks that try to fly, miss. Go limp. Fall like a sock that has given up on everything, and the basket will catch you out of pity.\"\n\nIt sniffs. \"Or jump. Add to my tally. See if I care.\"","choices":[{"label":"Go limp and flop toward the basket","to":"h_basket"},{"label":"Slide back down to the Duct","to":"u_vent_duct"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_cleaning","title":"The Annual Cleaning","art":"vacuum","text":"Nobody has time to explain. The light comes first. The whole dryer groans and scrapes forward as giant hands drag it from the wall, and daylight floods the Lint Kingdom for the first time in a year. Dust bunnies scatter, shrieking. The King is rolling in circles shouting \"ORDER! ORDER!\"\n\nThen the Long Hose descends. Its mouth is round and black and roaring, and it eats the far corner of the kingdom in one long slurp: pillars, bottle caps, three guards, a raisin.\n\nIt swings toward you. You can feel the pull in your cuff. Nearby, a bottle cap lies upturned like a little steel bunker. Behind you, the King has rolled into a corner and is trembling.","choices":[{"label":"Dive under the bottle cap","to":"u_hide"},{"label":"Stand and shield the King","to":"u_vacuumed"},{"label":"Run for the vent","to":"u_vent_duct"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_hide","title":"Under the Bottle Cap","art":"vacuum","text":"You dive under the bottle cap and pull your toe in tight. The roar passes overhead like a storm made of hunger. The cap rattles, lifts a terrible half inch, and drops back down. Then the roar moves on, and on, and stops.\n\nWhen you peek out, the Lint Kingdom is mostly gone. The floor is bare linoleum, shocking and cold. A giant hand reaches down and plucks up a stray sock nearby, then another, and tosses them into a basket.\n\nFrom the corner, King Fluffington wobbles out, much smaller than before. \"The kingdom,\" he whispers. \"We must rebuild.\" He looks at you. Over at the basket, the hand is reaching again.","choices":[{"label":"Stay and help the King rebuild","to":"u_heir"},{"label":"Let the hand find you","to":"h_basket"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"u_vacuumed","title":"The Long Hose","art":"garbage","text":"You plant yourself in front of King Fluffington and spread your cuff wide. \"Not today!\" you cry, which is very brave and also not how vacuums work.\n\nThe Long Hose takes you with a single *thwup*. You tumble through a dark tube, past screaming dust bunnies and a terrified paperclip, and land in the bag: a warm gray world of hair and crumbs and one Lego.\n\nIt is not so bad, actually. The King is in here too, and most of the court. You hold a small ceremony and crown nobody. On Thursday, the bag goes out to the curb, and you hear the truck coming, and you think: well. At least I was brave about it.","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"Into the Bag","kind":"bad"}},
{"id":"h_basket","title":"The Laundry Basket","art":"basket","text":"You land face down in a plastic laundry basket, which is the closest thing a sock has to a cathedral. Warm towels rise around you like bread. A fitted pillowcase snores. Everything smells of tumble dry low and somebody else's fabric softener.\n\nYou are in the House. Somewhere in this House is the top drawer of the dresser, and somewhere in that drawer is Pim, calm, patient Pim, probably folding and unfolding with worry. Or calmly not worrying. Pim was always better at that.\n\nAbove you, the basket's rim is a cliff of woven plastic holes. Far away, footsteps thump. The Owner is coming back for the basket, or isn't. With the Owner, you can never tell.","choices":[{"label":"Climb out over the rim","to":"h_basket_climb"},{"label":"Burrow in and wait to be carried","to":"h_carried"},{"label":"Notice this load smells a bit damp","to":"w_rinse"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_basket_climb","title":"Over the Rim","art":"basket","text":"You inchworm up the basket wall, hooking your toe through one plastic hole, then the next. It is slow. It is undignified. A dish towel watches and says nothing, which is somehow worse.\n\nAt the top you teeter, then tumble onto the cold tiles of the laundry room. The floor here is a desert of stray lint and one lonely bobby pin, staring at the ceiling as if it has seen things.\n\nAhead, the doorway opens onto the hallway. From somewhere close by comes a sound you know from Pim's stories: the jingle of a collar, and a tail thumping a wall in pure, uncomplicated joy.","choices":[{"label":"Creep out into the hallway","to":"h_hallway"},{"label":"Face the jingling sound","to":"h_biscuit"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_carried","title":"Basket Passenger","art":"stairs","text":"You burrow between a bath towel and a pair of flannel pajama pants and hold very still. The world lurches. Giant hands have lifted the basket, and you sway upward in the warm dark like a pea in a hot air balloon.\n\nThrough the plastic holes you glimpse the stairs going by: carpeted, endless, each step a cliff you would have needed an hour to climb. The Owner hums something tuneless. The pajama pants complain about the turbulence.\n\nNear the top, the basket tilts on the landing as the Owner adjusts their grip. One good wriggle and you could spill out. Or you could stay put and see where the folding happens.","choices":[{"label":"Ride on to wherever the folding is","to":"h_bed"},{"label":"Wriggle out onto the landing","to":"h_landing"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_door","title":"Back Indoors","art":"hallway","text":"One way or another, through the dog flap or past the Owner's giant feet, you make it indoors. You lie on the doormat, damp and gritty, breathing in the smell of indoors: toast, carpet, and the faint holy scent of a dryer somewhere down the hall.\n\nThe House. You made it back. Somewhere upstairs is the sock drawer, and in the sock drawer is Pim. You remember that much even if you have no idea where the stairs are.\n\nThe doormat bristles under you. It says WELCOME in letters it clearly does not mean. To your left is a long hallway. To your right, a living room with a couch so large it has its own weather. And something nearby is wagging.","choices":[{"label":"Head down the hallway","to":"h_hallway"},{"label":"Make for the couch","to":"h_couch"},{"label":"Investigate the wagging","to":"h_biscuit"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_hallway","title":"The Long Hallway","art":"hallway","text":"The hallway stretches ahead like a carpeted highway, all beige and hope. Dust motes drift in a sunbeam. You feel the faint vibration of the house: a fridge humming, a dryer settling, a clock that ticks slightly louder than it needs to.\n\nOn a radiator by the wall lies a gray cat, folded into a perfect loaf, one eye open and fixed on you with the expression of a professor grading a terrible essay.\n\nFurther along, a dog bed the size of a small nation sits in a corner, piled with suspicious treasures. And through the far doorway, the living room couch looms, its cushions slumped like sleeping hills.","choices":[{"label":"Approach the cat on the radiator","to":"h_professor"},{"label":"Peek at the dog bed","to":"h_biscuit"},{"label":"Head for the couch","to":"h_couch"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_biscuit","title":"Biscuit","art":"dog","text":"A golden wall of fur arrives. It has a nose, and the nose is very interested in you. It is wet. It is enormous. It huffs warm breath through your weave until your stripes stand on end.\n\nThis is Biscuit. Pim told you about Biscuit. Pim survived Biscuit, which was talked about in the drawer the way people talk about surviving a shipwreck. \"He doesn't mean any harm,\" Pim said. \"He just loves things with his whole mouth.\"\n\nBiscuit's tail goes off like a metronome set to *allegro*. He makes a small hopeful noise. He gently, gently, opens his mouth.","choices":[{"label":"Go limp and let him carry you","to":"h_biscuit_carry"},{"label":"Try to reason with him","to":"h_biscuit_talk"},{"label":"Bolt for the radiator","to":"h_professor"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_biscuit_carry","title":"In the Jaws of Love","art":"dog","text":"Biscuit picks you up with a softness that should not be possible for a mouth that size. You dangle from his lip, swaying, damp but unharmed. He is very proud of you. He trots in a little circle to show you to nobody in particular.\n\nThen he sets off with purpose. You pass the hallway, a shoe, a table leg. Ahead, two paths: a corner where his bed waits, heaped with treasures, and the rattling dog flap, beyond which lies grass and sky and everything a dog considers important.\n\nHe pauses, considering. You could kick and tip the balance. Or you could hang on and see.","choices":[{"label":"Kick toward the dog flap","to":"o_yard"},{"label":"Go limp and let him choose the bed","to":"h_dogbed"},{"label":"Get spotted, slobbery, by the Owner","to":"w_rinse"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_biscuit_talk","title":"A Reasonable Dog","art":"dog","text":"\"Biscuit,\" you say, very firmly for a sock. \"Sit.\"\n\nHe sits. He sits so fast and so hard that his whole body wiggles with the effort. You did not expect that to work. Neither, from his face, did he.\n\nYou tell him about Pim. The right sock. Lilac, calm, smells like cedar from the drawer. Biscuit's ears lift at \"Pim.\" He knows that smell. He whines softly and points his nose at the stairs, then back at you, then at the stairs, in case you missed it.\n\n\"Will you help me?\" you ask. His tail sweeps the floor clean in reply. He would like to help. He would also, very much, like to show you his bed.","choices":[{"label":"Follow his nose to the stairs","to":"h_stairs"},{"label":"Agree to see his treasures first","to":"h_dogbed"}],"ending":null,"flag":"biscuit_friend"},
{"id":"h_dogbed","title":"The Hoard","art":"dog","text":"Biscuit's bed is a plaid crater lined with dog hair and treasure. You are laid tenderly on top, beside a tennis ball, a slipper with a heroic chew history, and three other socks.\n\nThe socks look up, blissful and a bit soggy. \"Oh, you'll love it here,\" says a blue ankle sock. \"He sleeps with his nose on us. We're the most loved things in the house.\" A sports sock with no elastic left nods slowly, like a monk.\n\nBiscuit circles three times and flops down, chin across you, sighing a sigh of total contentment. It is warm. It is so warm. You think of Pim, and the cedar drawer, and how far away the stairs are.","choices":[{"label":"Stay. Just for a while.","to":"h_end_beloved"},{"label":"Wait for him to sleep, then sneak off","to":"h_hallway"},{"label":"Let the Owner tidy up the hoard","to":"j_drawer"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_end_beloved","title":"Biscuit's Favorite","art":"dog","text":"A while becomes an afternoon. An afternoon becomes a season. You never quite decide to stay, you just keep not leaving.\n\nBiscuit carries you everywhere: to the door when the Owner comes home, to the yard to show the squirrels, back to bed every night with the care of a dog transporting a crown jewel. Your stripes fade to a gentle swamp color. Your darn becomes a hole, and the hole becomes his favorite spot to hold you by.\n\nSometimes, late at night, you smell cedar on the Owner's feet as they pass, and you think of Pim, and something in your weave aches. Then Biscuit sighs in his sleep and pulls you closer, and you are, if not matched, at least completely and ridiculously loved.","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"Biscuit's Favorite","kind":"bittersweet"}},
{"id":"h_professor","title":"The Professor","art":"cat","text":"The gray cat on the radiator does not move when you approach. Cats never move when you want them to. He simply lets you get closer, and closer, until you are near enough to see your own reflection in one disdainful green eye.\n\n\"A left sock,\" he says, as if diagnosing something. \"Striped. Darned. Lost. How wonderfully predictable.\" His name, you learn, is Professor, though he does not say what he is a professor of. Presumably everything.\n\nHe yawns, showing teeth you try not to think about. \"Let me guess. You want the drawer. Everyone wants the drawer. Nobody ever asks *me* what I want.\"","choices":[{"label":"Ask him what he wants","to":"h_professor_deal"},{"label":"Leave him to his radiator","to":"h_couch"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_professor_deal","title":"Terms and Conditions","art":"cat","text":"Professor's tail flicks once, a gavel. \"What I want,\" he says, \"is a hair tie. Black. Elastic. Excellent for batting under the refrigerator. The Owner had one this morning and it is now in the couch, as all good things eventually are.\"\n\n\"Bring it to me,\" he continues, \"and I will tell you exactly where the drawer is and how to get into it. Exactly. Not the vague nonsense the dryer sheets peddle.\"\n\nYou point out that you could just find the drawer yourself. Professor closes his eyes. \"You could,\" he agrees. \"And I could watch. I enjoy a tragedy.\"","choices":[{"label":"Go into the couch for the hair tie","to":"h_couch_market"},{"label":"Find the drawer yourself","to":"h_stairs"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_couch","title":"Couch Country","art":"couch","text":"The couch is a range of three great cushions slumped against each other like tired giants. You climb, hook by hook, and stand on the summit plateau, looking down into the cracks between.\n\nDown there, something is happening. You hear the clink of coins, the rustle of a receipt, and many small voices haggling. The cushion cracks are not empty. They are an economy.\n\nFurther down still, in the deepest crack at the corner, the darkness has a strange softness to it. It does not look like a gap. It looks like a fold in something much bigger than a couch.","choices":[{"label":"Descend into the cushion market","to":"h_couch_market"},{"label":"Peer into the deepest crack","to":"h_couch_deep"},{"label":"Climb down and seek the stairs","to":"h_stairs"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_couch_market","title":"The Crumb Exchange","art":"couch","text":"Between the cushions lies a bustling bazaar. Crumbs are the currency, sorted by grade: toast, cracker, and the legendary Cheeto dust, which is traded only by the very rich. A pen cap runs the bank. A lone popcorn kernel sells fortunes nobody believes.\n\nYou find the hair tie being used as a corral for three runaway raisins. The pen cap negotiates hard. In the end you trade a single strand of your own wool, which the market treats like gold thread, and the hair tie is yours.\n\n\"Taking it to the cat?\" the pen cap asks. \"Smart. That cat pays in information, and his information is always right. Annoyingly so.\"","choices":[{"label":"Carry the hair tie to Professor","to":"h_professor_map"},{"label":"Explore deeper below the market","to":"h_couch_deep"},{"label":"Get scooped up by a rummaging hand","to":"j_drawer"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_couch_deep","title":"The Deep Cushion","art":"portal","text":"You crawl past the market, past the last crumb, past a TV remote battery that has been down here so long it has become a sort of hermit. The cushion gap narrows, then softens, then stops being a gap at all.\n\nThe fabric here feels like the inside of a cloud. It shimmers faintly pastel, lavender and mint, and you hear something impossible: the far-off, gentle rumble of a spin cycle that is not coming from any machine in this house.\n\nA warm draft curls out of the fold and smells, overwhelmingly, of being found. Your stripes tingle. Something in there is calling you, kindly. Something in there may not want to give you back.","choices":[{"label":"Slip through the soft fold","to":"x_portal"},{"label":"Claw back up toward the light","to":"h_couch"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_professor_map","title":"The Professor's Map","art":"cat","text":"You drop the hair tie at Professor's paws. He regards it for a long time, then bats it once, lazily, and something in his face almost becomes pleasure before he catches it.\n\n\"Very well,\" he says. \"Up the stairs. Through the second door, the one that sticks. The dresser is beside the window. Top drawer. Your Pim is at the back on the left, behind a pair of wool hiking socks with ideas above their station. The drawer sticks. Push from the bottom right corner.\"\n\nHe flicks an ear toward the laundry basket by the foot of the stairs, then pauses. \"And if you reach the foot of that dresser and find yourself stuck, which you will, call for me. I may come. I may not. It depends how bored I am.\"","choices":[{"label":"Head for the stairs","to":"h_stairs"},{"label":"Hop into the basket for a ride up","to":"h_basket"}],"ending":null,"flag":"knows_drawer"},
{"id":"h_stairs","title":"The Mountain","art":"stairs","text":"The stairs rise above you, fourteen carpeted cliffs climbing toward a landing lost in shadow. Each step is the height of three of you stacked. Mountaineers would weep. Mountaineers would at least have thumbs.\n\nAt the base you find a lost earring back and a fallen cheerio, the bones of previous expeditions. You consider the climb. You consider your darn, which is not built for this.\n\nBehind you, a laundry basket waits on the floor, half full and abandoned for now. And somewhere in the house, a large and friendly dog is listening for his name.","choices":[{"label":"Begin the climb","to":"h_stairs_climb"},{"label":"Whistle for Biscuit","to":"h_landing","requires":"biscuit_friend"},{"label":"Hide in the basket for a lift","to":"h_basket"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_stairs_climb","title":"The Ascent","art":"stairs","text":"Step one: you fling your toe over the edge and haul yourself up by carpet fibers. Step two: the same, but sweatier. By step six you have developed a technique, a sort of desperate cartwheel, and by step nine you have developed opinions about whoever invented stairs.\n\nOn step eleven, a gust from the heating vent nearly takes you. You cling to the carpet, flapping like a flag on a summit, and think of Pim. Pim would say, *one more step, then another.* Pim was always saying things like that. It was very annoying. You miss it terribly.\n\nThree steps to go. Your arms, which you do not have, ache.","choices":[{"label":"Push on to the landing","to":"h_landing"},{"label":"Let go and slide back down","to":"h_hallway"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_landing","title":"The Landing","art":"hallway","text":"You flop onto the landing, gasping lint. You have conquered the mountain, one way or another. Nobody will ever believe it. That is fine. That is how the greatest climbs always go.\n\nThe upstairs hallway is quieter, darker, carpeted in a deeper shade of beige. Doors line it. One is open just a crack, and through it you catch a sliver of dresser and a curtain moving in the breeze.\n\nBeside you, a window stands propped open. A breeze pours through it, rich with grass and sky, tugging at your stripes. One good puff and you would be out and gone, over the sill and down into the yard, all your hard climbing undone.","choices":[{"label":"Go through the door to the dresser","to":"h_bedroom"},{"label":"Let the breeze take you","to":"o_yard"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_bedroom","title":"The Bedroom","art":"bedroom","text":"The bedroom is vast and soft and smells of the Owner: sleep, shampoo, and a faint undertone of cedar that makes your whole weave sit up straight. Cedar. The drawer.\n\nThere it is: the dresser, standing by the window like a wooden cathedral, its brass handles gleaming. The top drawer is open a finger's width. A sliver of darkness. Somewhere in that darkness, Pim.\n\nAcross the room, the bed is heaped with clean laundry. The Owner sits on its edge, folding with the slow exhaustion of someone who has done this a thousand times and will do it a thousand more. Pairs go into neat balls. Singles go into a sad little pile.","choices":[{"label":"Cross to the foot of the dresser","to":"h_drawer"},{"label":"Climb onto the bed with the laundry","to":"h_bed"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_bed","title":"The Folding Table","art":"bedroom","text":"The bed is a snowfield of laundry. Shirts lie flat like sleeping seals. Towels are rolled into logs. And the Owner's hands move through it all like weather systems, sweeping, folding, pairing.\n\nYou see socks being matched. A pair of gray work socks, reunited. A pair of pink fuzzy ones, giddy. The Owner tucks each pair into a ball and drops it into a growing heap, destined for the drawer.\n\nThe singles sit in a pile at the foot of the bed, trying not to look hopeful. Among them is a beige argyle golf sock with a confident air. He sees you and winks. The hands, sweeping closer, have not noticed you yet.","choices":[{"label":"Lie flat and wait to be folded","to":"h_fold"},{"label":"Slide off the bed toward the dresser","to":"h_drawer"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_fold","title":"The Tired Hands","art":"bedroom","text":"The hands find you. They lift you to a height that makes your stitches swim. Somewhere far above, the Owner squints, sighs, and rummages through the pile of singles for something, anything, that looks right.\n\nYou try to will them toward the drawer. Toward Pim. But the Owner is tired, it is late, and the hands close instead on the beige argyle golf sock.\n\nThey hold you side by side. You are teal and mustard and anxious. He is beige and diamond-patterned and smells faintly of a country club. The Owner considers you both for one long moment, shrugs, and begins to roll you together into a ball.","choices":[{"label":"Go along with it","to":"h_end_wrong"},{"label":"Wriggle loose and run for the dresser","to":"h_drawer"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_end_wrong","title":"Close Enough","art":"mismatch","text":"You end up in the drawer, rolled up with Gerald. That is the golf sock's name. He tells you within the first four seconds.\n\nAt first you are horrified. Then, slowly, you are not. Gerald turns out to be a wonderful listener. He has been to Scotland, or at least to a putting green near the highway. He thinks your darn is \"distinguished.\" When you mention Pim, he nods and says, \"Ah. A first love.\" And then, kindly, he changes the subject.\n\nThe Owner wears you together on Tuesdays. People stare at their ankles. The Owner does not care, and after a while, neither do you. It is not the pair you were bought in. It is, it turns out, a pair. Somewhere at the back of the drawer, you hope, Pim has found a Gerald too.","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"Close Enough","kind":"weird"}},
{"id":"h_drawer","title":"The Foot of the Dresser","art":"drawer","text":"You stand at the foot of the dresser and look up. And up. And up. It is a wooden cliff with four brass handles bolted to it like the rungs of a ladder built for giants.\n\nAt the very top, the drawer is open a crack. Cedar breathes out of it. And faintly, so faintly you might be imagining it, you hear a familiar voice humming the little tune Pim always hums on wash day, the one that goes nowhere and comforts everyone.\n\nYou are so close your stripes hurt. The handles glint. Behind you, footsteps creak closer with an armful of laundry. And on the windowsill, a gray tail twitches, very deliberately, in your direction.","choices":[{"label":"Climb the brass handles","to":"h_handles"},{"label":"Wait for the Owner's hands","to":"h_hands"},{"label":"Call to the cat on the windowsill","to":"h_professor_lift","requires":"knows_drawer"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_handles","title":"The Brass Ladder","art":"drawer","text":"You throw yourself at the bottom handle and cling. Brass is cold and slippery, with nothing for your weave to grip but a few tiny scratches. You use every one of them.\n\nHandle one. A swing, a lunge, a terrible moment of hanging by your heel. Handle two. Handle three, where you rest, panting, and look down at a carpet that seems as far away as the moon.\n\nThen the last one. Above it, the drawer's open crack is right there, a dark strip smelling of cedar and home, barely wider than you are. It is the kind of drawer that sticks. To reach it you will have to let go of the handle completely and fling yourself upward, trusting to static and nerve.","choices":[{"label":"Fling yourself through the gap","to":"h_inside"},{"label":"Push at the bottom right corner","to":"h_inside","requires":"knows_drawer"},{"label":"Climb back down and rethink","to":"h_drawer"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_hands","title":"The Giant Hands","art":"bedroom","text":"The footsteps stop. A shadow falls. Then the hands descend, huge and warm, and pluck you off the carpet as if you weigh nothing, which, to them, you do.\n\nYou are held up to the light. The Owner turns you over. Your stripes. Your red heel. Your darn, oh no, your darn, which they examine for a long, terrible moment while a thumb rubs over the stitches.\n\n\"Huh,\" says the Owner, in the voice they use for things they are deciding about. One hand drifts toward the dresser. The other hand drifts toward the rag bin by the door. You could hold perfectly still and look worthy, or you could panic.","choices":[{"label":"Hold still and look worthy","to":"h_inside"},{"label":"Wriggle in panic","to":"h_end_rag"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_end_rag","title":"The Rag Life","art":"rag","text":"You wriggle. You thrash. You flail with every fiber you have. The Owner watches you squirm in their hand and laughs, because to a person, a wriggling sock looks exactly like a puppet.\n\nFirst come the googly eyes, glued on crooked. Then a mouth drawn in marker. For one week you are \"Mister Stripes\" and you perform a dramatic reading of the grocery list. Then the novelty fades, the glue fails, and you are demoted to the cleaning cupboard.\n\nNow you dust the baseboards on Saturdays, one googly eye still clinging on, staring at the dresser you can never quite reach. The other rags are kind. Their stories all start the same way: \"I was a sock, once.\"","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"The Rag Life","kind":"bad"}},
{"id":"h_professor_lift","title":"The Professor Is Bored","art":"cat","text":"\"Please,\" you call up to the windowsill, feeling ridiculous.\n\nNothing. Then a long, theatrical sigh from the windowsill. The gray cat pours himself down to the carpet like spilled ink and stands over you. \"I want it noted,\" he says, \"that I am doing this for my own entertainment and for no other reason.\"\n\nHe takes you gently by the cuff with his teeth. Then he coils, and leaps, and you are flying past the handles, past the second drawer, up into the cedar-scented air. He lands on the dresser top without a sound, dips his head, and drops you neatly into the open crack.\n\n\"Do try not to get lost again,\" he says. \"It is tedious for everyone.\"","choices":[{"label":"Look for Pim in the dark","to":"h_inside"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_inside","title":"The Sock Drawer","art":"drawer","text":"You tumble into the drawer and land on a pillow of folded cotton. It is dark, and close, and smells so strongly of cedar that you sneeze.\n\nAround you the drawer society stirs. Balled-up pairs peer at you, murmuring. A pair of Christmas socks, out of season and sulking. Two wool hiking socks who have seen mountains, real ones, and would like you to know it. A pair of nylons tangled in permanent argument. \"A single,\" someone whispers, the way you would say *a ghost.*\n\nYou wriggle past them, behind the hiking socks, toward the back left corner by the knot in the wood where the light never reaches. Your heart, which socks do not technically have, is hammering. Someone back there has stopped humming. Someone back there is listening.","choices":[{"label":"Call out for Pim","to":"h_matched"},{"label":"Creep forward in silence","to":"h_matched"},{"label":"Make the entrance this trip deserves","to":"h_secret_full_load","requires":["has_quarter","met_mitt"]}],"ending":null},
{"id":"h_matched","title":"Matched","art":"reunion","text":"\"Stripes?\" There, in the back left corner, folded neatly in half, is Pim. Lilac, soft, with the calm of a sock who once survived a dog, except that Pim's toe is trembling. \"You're late,\" says Pim. \"I wasn't worried.\" It is the first lie Pim has ever told, and it is a terrible one.\n\n\"I got lost,\" you say. \"Then a lot of other things happened. Some of it you won't believe.\" And Pim says, \"I kept your spot,\" and shuffles over, and there it is: a warm hollow in the cedar dark, exactly your shape, defended all afternoon against the hiking socks, the Christmas socks, and one very persistent nylon.\n\nYou slide in. It fits. Of course it fits. You came home in the same paper band, a striped one and a lilac one, the only pair in the three-pack that made no sense, and the Owner rolled you together on the very first day and never once thought better of it. \"Your darn looks good,\" Pim says. \"It does not.\" \"It does. It looks like you've been somewhere.\"\n\nIn the morning, the Owner will open the drawer, reach in without looking, and pull out a pair. Two socks, rolled together, warm, ordinary, complete. Nobody will ever know what it took. That is the best part.","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"Matched","kind":"good"}},
{"id":"h_secret_full_load","title":"The Full Load","art":"reunion","text":"You do not creep. You tip your cuff, and out rolls Vernon, who has ridden there the whole way, give or take a few transactions he would rather not discuss. He spins across the cedar like a tiny drumroll. \"Gentlesocks! Hiking socks!\" he booms. \"Presenting the left sock, home from the Drum and points beyond!\"\n\nThe whole drawer sits up. The nylons stop arguing mid-word. From the back left corner, a lilac toe pokes out of the dark, and Pim says, \"You brought a quarter.\" \"I brought a friend,\" you say. \"He's a quarter.\"\n\nThen Vernon has news, because the vents talk and quarters listen. Mitt went looking for Mott and found her in the Odd Sock Dimension, flour to the cuff, running the best bakery in the place. \"Sent you a cinnamon lint roll,\" says Vernon. \"I ate it. Don't ask how.\"\n\nYou slide in beside Pim. It fits. Vernon settles between you like a coin in a warm pocket. Everyone accounted for. A full load, at last.","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"The Full Load","kind":"good"}},
{"id":"o_vent","title":"Blown Out","art":"vent_duct","text":"The duct coughs. There is no other word for it. One moment you are drifting through ribbed aluminum dark, the next you are fired out of a little hinged flap like a party popper and slapped against the side of the house.\n\nDaylight. Actual sky, enormous and blue and frankly showing off. You cling to the vinyl siding by a thread of static, flapping beside the vent cap, which wheezes warm air past you and smells faintly of fabric softener and regret.\n\nBelow you, a flowerbed. Beyond it, a yard, a clothesline, a whole outdoors that has never once been tumble dried. The breeze tugs at your cuff, curious.","choices":[{"label":"Let the wind take you","to":"o_wind"},{"label":"Drop into the flowerbed","to":"o_flowerbed"},{"label":"Wriggle back into the vent","to":"u_gate"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_yard","title":"The Back Yard","art":"yard","text":"You land in grass. Wet grass, which is a betrayal of everything a freshly dried sock stands for. Whatever got you out here, a dog with priorities or a breeze with no manners, has already wandered off to bother something more interesting. The house has let you go.\n\nYou take stock. Teal and mustard stripes: present. Red heel: damp. The darn on your toe: still embarrassing. Above you the sky goes on forever, which seems excessive.\n\nThe back of the house looms, its dog flap swinging gently. To one side, a clothesline sags with pinned laundry, murmuring. And under the hedge, something small and twitchy is watching you. It has clearly been watching for a while.","choices":[{"label":"Inchworm toward the dog flap","to":"h_door"},{"label":"Approach the clothesline","to":"o_clothesline"},{"label":"Investigate the twitchy thing","to":"o_squirrel"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_wind","title":"Airborne","art":"yard","text":"You let go. The wind takes you the way the dryer never could: no drum, no walls, just lift. You tumble over the flowerbed, over the fence, wool fibers singing. It is the single most frightening and wonderful thing that has ever happened to a sock, and you have been through a heavy-duty cycle.\n\nThe wind does not talk, exactly, but it has moods. Right now its mood is *wherever*. You spin past the clothesline, where a pair of boxer shorts gasps at you. Below, the street glitters with puddles. Above, a black shape with glossy wings tilts her head and follows.\n\nYou have a few seconds of steering, if wriggling counts as steering.","choices":[{"label":"Wriggle down to the clothesline","to":"o_clothesline"},{"label":"Ride it out over the street","to":"o_street"},{"label":"Snag on the big oak branch","to":"o_crow"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_flowerbed","title":"The Hydrangeas","art":"yard","text":"You drop into mulch, which turns out to be wood chips that have given up on being trees. A hydrangea towers over you, blue and judgmental. A beetle trundles past without so much as a hello.\n\nThe flowerbed runs along the foundation of the house like a moat, and moats, you reason, lead to doors. Somewhere ahead, around the corner, you can hear the dog flap creak as Biscuit comes and goes. Above you, the vent cap still breathes warm laundry air, close enough to reach if you climb the downspout bracket.\n\nThen a shadow crosses the mulch. Something with wings has noticed the bright stripes in the flowerbed.","choices":[{"label":"Follow the foundation to the door","to":"h_door"},{"label":"Climb back up into the vent","to":"u_gate"},{"label":"Freeze and hope the shadow passes","to":"o_crow"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_clothesline","title":"The Clothesline","art":"clothesline","text":"Up close, the clothesline is a society. A long row of laundry hangs from wooden pins, and every item is talking at once. A bedsheet complains about the humidity. Two dish towels argue about the proper fold. A pair of jeans, hanging upside down by the cuffs, insists this is how jeans are *meant* to dry and everyone else is doing it wrong.\n\n\"A loose sock!\" cries a pillowcase. \"On the *ground*! Have you no pin?\"\n\nThe line goes quiet. Forty garments regard you with the shared horror of the respectably clipped. Then a faded beach towel, clearly some kind of elder, clears her throat. \"Let the creature speak.\"","choices":[{"label":"Ask the line for help","to":"o_council"},{"label":"Ask to be pinned up with them","to":"o_pinned"},{"label":"Back away toward the hedge","to":"o_squirrel"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_council","title":"The Council of the Line","art":"clothesline","text":"The beach towel, whose name is Margarita Sunset, convenes a council. This takes a while. The jeans want a vote. The dish towels want two votes. A bra abstains on principle.\n\nAt last the verdict: the line knows two ways home. \"The Owner takes us in at sundown,\" says Margarita. \"Pin yourself among us and ride the basket through the back door. Or go across the street. The laundromat socks know every route there is. Rough lot, mind you. They have a gang.\"\n\nA scarf at the end of the line murmurs, \"A child's mitten blew through here last winter, off the sledding hill. Red, a right hand. Mott, she said her name was. Took the laundromat road. Never came back.\" The whole line shudders together, as if a breeze went through it, which it did.","choices":[{"label":"Get pinned and wait for sundown","to":"o_pinned"},{"label":"Head for the street","to":"o_street"},{"label":"Tell them about Mitt","to":"o_mott","requires":"met_mitt"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_mott","title":"A Message on the Line","art":"mitten","text":"\"Mitt?\" The scarf goes very still. \"A mitten with a string? Talks fast? Calls stealing *redistribution*?\" You nod. The whole line rustles at once.\n\n\"Mott asked after it,\" the scarf says softly. \"All the way down the line, peg by peg. She said if a mitten called Mitt ever came looking, it should know she went the soft way: across to the laundromat, behind the very last dryer, Number Nine, where the wall goes fuzzy and smells of warm cinnamon.\"\n\nMargarita Sunset sniffs. \"Cinnamon,\" she says darkly. \"Nobody comes back from cinnamon.\"\n\nYou think of Mitt alone in the drum since February, pretending not to care. A message is a warm thing too, and you happen to be built for carrying warm things.","choices":[{"label":"Find Number Nine and lean on the fuzzy wall","to":"x_portal"},{"label":"Get pinned and carry the news home","to":"o_pinned"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_pinned","title":"Pinned","art":"clothesline","text":"A helpful clothespin, who has clearly been waiting all its life to be asked, scissors open and clamps your cuff to the line. You hang between a striped dish towel and a sock-shaped silence where the line's last sock used to be. Nobody mentions it.\n\nHanging is peaceful. The sun warms your stripes. The wind rocks you. The jeans tell a long story about a rodeo they insist they attended. You learn more about humidity than any sock should know.\n\nThen two things happen at once. The back door opens and the Owner comes out with the laundry basket. And a crow lands on the line post, eyeing your bright stripes with naked greed.","choices":[{"label":"Ride the basket in, tumble onto the doormat","to":"h_door"},{"label":"Hiss at the crow","to":"o_crow"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_squirrel","title":"The Squirrel","art":"squirrel","text":"Something small and twitchy is waiting under the hedge. It is a squirrel. It approaches in a series of panicked lunges, as if every step is a fresh decision it regrets. Its tail is magnificent. Its eyes are two small black planets of pure anxiety.\n\n\"You're soft,\" it says. \"Are you a nest? You look like a nest. I need a nest. Winter's coming. Winter is always coming. Do you know how many acorns I've lost? I don't. That's the problem.\"\n\nIt introduces itself as Pocket. Pocket would like to stuff you full of acorns and bury you, which is unacceptable. But Pocket also climbs everything, sees everything, and knows every way into the house that a small creature could use.","choices":[{"label":"Offer Pocket a deal","to":"o_squirrel_deal"},{"label":"Flee toward the street","to":"o_gutter"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_squirrel_deal","title":"A Deal with Pocket","art":"squirrel","text":"You negotiate like a sock who has watched the Owner haggle at a yard sale. Pocket may keep three acorns in your toe for the journey. In exchange, transport. Pocket agrees so fast you suspect you undersold yourself.\n\nRiding a squirrel is like riding a sneeze. Pocket hauls you up the oak in jerks and bursts, chattering a running monologue about hawks and the weather and a bird feeder that betrayed it in 2023. From the top you see everything: the roof, the street, the laundromat sign blinking across the way, the dog flap swinging below.\n\n\"Where to?\" Pocket asks, already forgetting the acorns.","choices":[{"label":"Down to the dog flap, please","to":"h_door"},{"label":"The roof, please. I'll take it from there","to":"o_roof"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_crow","title":"Margo","art":"crow","text":"She lands beside you with the confidence of a bird who owns several spoons. Glossy black, one eye milky, the other bright as a new button. She tilts her head and looks at you the way a jeweler looks at a ring.\n\n\"Stripes,\" she says. \"Teal *and* mustard. And is that a darn? Hand-mended. Very rare.\" You did not know your darn was an asset. You are briefly flattered, then remember that crows have nests and nests need lining.\n\n\"I'm Margo,\" she says. \"I collect. You'd be the soft centerpiece of my whole east wing. But I'm a reasonable bird. Everything has a price, and everything can be carried.\"","choices":[{"label":"Negotiate with her","to":"o_bargain"},{"label":"Let Vernon do the talking","to":"o_vernon","requires":"has_quarter"},{"label":"Play dead","to":"o_nest"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_vernon","title":"Vernon's Pitch","art":"coin","text":"Vernon has been rattling in your cuff this whole time, and now he rolls out onto your stripes and catches the sun like a tiny silver stage light. Margo's good eye goes perfectly round.\n\n\"Lady,\" he says, \"put the beak away. You don't want me. I'm one quarter. Small potatoes. But I know where the *rest* of us live.\" And he tells her about the couch: the deep cushions, the economy of crumbs and coins, a lost treasury of dimes, a button shaped like a star, a key nobody has missed in years.\n\nMargo leans in so far she nearly tips over. \"Delaware,\" she murmurs, reading him. \"1999. Classy.\"\n\n\"Classy and *connected*,\" says Vernon. \"One ride for the kid, and the address is yours.\" Margo is already spreading her wings.","choices":[{"label":"Go before she changes her mind","to":"o_flight"},{"label":"Ask for a quick drop at the back door","to":"h_door"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_bargain","title":"Terms","art":"crow","text":"\"A ride,\" you say. \"Anywhere I want. In exchange for...\" You think hard. You have nothing. You are a sock.\n\nMargo waits, patient as a gargoyle. \"Something shiny,\" she suggests. \"I'm not greedy. A bottle cap. A paperclip with character.\" You describe the gap behind the dryer in lavish detail: bottle caps, a hairpin, an earring glinting in the lint. Whatever you have not seen yourself, you embroider. You are, after all, made of thread. Margo's good eye widens until it is almost round.\n\n\"Behind the dryer,\" she breathes. \"Fine. I'll fly you where you like, and you tell me how to get in there.\" She pauses. \"If you're lying, you become insulation.\"","choices":[{"label":"Shake on it (with your cuff)","to":"o_flight"},{"label":"Refuse. Socks have principles","to":"o_nest"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_nest","title":"The Nest","art":"crow","text":"Margo carries you up anyway, because crows take neither no nor silence for an answer. Her nest sits in the crook of the big oak, a vast woven bowl of twigs and stolen glory: a key, three spoons, a chess knight, a tangle of tinsel, half a pair of sunglasses, and a single baby shoe that refuses to discuss its past.\n\nShe tucks you along the bottom with real tenderness. \"There,\" she says. \"Now everything is soft.\" Then she flaps off to find something to go with your colors.\n\nThe baby shoe clears its throat. \"She's gone till dusk,\" it whispers. \"You could climb out. Down the trunk, onto the roof. Or you could stay. It's warm here. It's quiet. Nobody ever loses you twice.\"","choices":[{"label":"Climb out onto the roof","to":"o_roof"},{"label":"Stay. It is very warm","to":"o_lining"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_lining","title":"Nest Egg","art":"nest","text":"You stay. You tell yourself it is just until the wind dies down, and then just until spring, and then you stop telling yourself anything at all.\n\nIn April, Margo lays three speckled eggs on top of you. You keep them warm. You were made to keep things warm; it is the one job a sock is truly built for, and you are good at it. The chicks hatch ugly and loud and perfect, and they sleep on your teal stripes and peck at your darn as though it is the most interesting thing in the world.\n\nSometimes, on windy nights, you think of Pim in the drawer, calm as always, waiting. You hope Pim found another left sock. You hope Pim is warm too.","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"Nest Egg","kind":"bittersweet"}},
{"id":"o_flight","title":"Crow Air","art":"crow","text":"Margo grips you gently in her beak, and the yard drops away. This is nothing like drifting on a breeze. It is purposeful, steered, and slightly drooly. From up here, the house is a box of secrets. You can see the vent cap still puffing, the dog flap, the street, and the blinking laundromat sign.\n\n\"Mmmff?\" says Margo, which you interpret as *where to*.\n\nIf you know which window is the bedroom, you could point. If not, there are plenty of other places a crow can drop a sock.","choices":[{"label":"Drop me at the back door","to":"h_door"},{"label":"The bedroom window, top floor","to":"h_drawer","requires":"knows_drawer"},{"label":"Across the street, to the laundromat","to":"o_laundromat"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_roof","title":"The Roof","art":"rooftop","text":"The roof is a hot, gritty desert of shingles, and you are the only sock to have ever crossed it. Probably. You suspect a frisbee was here once; it has left a sad circle in the moss.\n\nFrom up here you can see the gutter running along the edge, choked with leaves and rainwater, and beyond it the downspout plunging toward the street. Clouds are piling up in the west with the grim purpose of a rinse cycle. Wind snaps at your cuff, eager for another go.\n\nYou could slide. You could jump. You could wait for the weather to decide for you, which is how most of your life has gone so far.","choices":[{"label":"Slide down the downspout","to":"o_gutter"},{"label":"Jump and let the wind catch you","to":"o_wind"},{"label":"Climb the old TV antenna","to":"o_windsock"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_windsock","title":"Windsock","art":"rooftop","text":"The antenna is a rusted metal tree that has not received a channel since before you were knitted. You inchworm up it anyway, and at the very top the wind finds your open cuff, rushes in, and fills you like a lung.\n\nYou stand straight out. Rigid. Magnificent. A sock at full attention, pointing due east.\n\nYou never come down. You don't want to. Every morning the neighborhood looks up to see which way you are blowing. The crow in the oak plans her flights by you. The squirrel under the hedge checks you before every acorn run. Clotheslines up and down the street pin their washing by your forecast, and even the bedsheets stop complaining about the humidity.\n\nOn still nights you droop, and remember being a pair. Then the breeze comes back, and you have work to do.","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"Windsock","kind":"weird"}},
{"id":"o_gutter","title":"Rain","art":"drain","text":"The rain comes all at once, as if someone upstairs pressed *heavy duty*. Within seconds you are soaked through, heavier than you have ever been, cold wash and no one asked you first. Water gathers you up and carries you along the curb in a brown rushing stream full of leaves and a single determined popsicle stick.\n\n\"First time?\" the popsicle stick shouts over the roar. \"Just go with it! It all ends up somewhere!\"\n\nAhead, the stream pours toward an iron grate set in the road. The storm drain. It gurgles like a giant laughing in its sleep. Your heel catches briefly on the edge of the curb.","choices":[{"label":"Let the current carry you on","to":"o_drain"},{"label":"Haul yourself up onto the curb","to":"o_street"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_drain","title":"The Storm Drain","art":"drain","text":"The current flings you at the iron grate, and you catch there, hanging by one damp thread. Below, the dark echoes with water and something else: voices. Hundreds of small, cheerful voices, echoing up from the tunnels.\n\n\"Come on down!\" they call. \"The water's fine! We're going to the sea!\" A hair tie. A sandwich bag. A glove that waves one finger at you. All of them lost, all of them strangely, unreasonably happy about it.\n\nThe thread holding you trembles. Up on the street, the rain is easing, and the laundromat sign is buzzing awake across the road. Down below, the whole wide world is flowing somewhere you have never been. You think of Pim. You think of the sea.","choices":[{"label":"Let go","to":"o_sea"},{"label":"Climb back up to the street","to":"o_street"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_sea","title":"Adventure Sock","art":"sea","text":"You let go. The dark takes you, then the tunnels, then a culvert, a creek, a river that smells of rust and frogs. For weeks you travel with the glove and the hair tie, telling stories, losing count of the spin cycles.\n\nAnd then, one morning, the sea. It is the biggest drum you have ever seen, and it never buzzes, and it never stops. You ride swells the color of your own teal stripes. A gull mistakes you for a fish and apologizes. You wash up on a beach, then wash off again, then wash up somewhere else entirely.\n\nYou never go back to the drawer. You are something else now: a legend among lost things, the striped sock who saw the ocean. Somewhere far inland, you hope Pim feels a sudden, inexplicable taste of salt.","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"Adventure Sock","kind":"weird"}},
{"id":"o_street","title":"The Street","art":"yard","text":"The street is wider than the whole laundry room, and it is not interested in you. Cars thunder past with a sound like a thousand dryers full of sneakers. A shopping bag cartwheels by and does not stop to chat. Road grit is already working its way into your stripes.\n\nOn one side, the front walk of your own house leads up to the porch, where the front door stands ajar. The Owner is carrying in groceries, giant feet going back and forth, a giant hand swinging low with every trip. On the other side, a laundromat hums behind fogged glass. Its sign reads SUDS CITY, with the second S flickering.\n\nYou can feel its dryers from here, a deep vibration in your fibers, like a choir of drums all humming your name.","choices":[{"label":"Scurry up the walk to the door","to":"h_door"},{"label":"Cross to the laundromat","to":"o_laundromat"},{"label":"Get scooped up, gritty, by the Owner","to":"w_rinse"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_laundromat","title":"Suds City","art":"laundromat","text":"You slip in under the door with a gust and a gum wrapper. Inside, the air is thick and warm and smells of forty brands of detergent at once. Rows of industrial dryers spin like planets, each one ten times the size of your home dryer. You feel very much from the suburbs.\n\nSocks are everywhere. Socks under the folding tables. Socks along the baseboards. Socks from a hundred different households, in every color and fiber, and none of them look lost. They look like they *live* here.\n\nIn one corner sits a cardboard box labeled LOST + FOUND in faded marker. In another, by the change machine, a cluster of hard-looking socks has gone quiet and turned to stare at you.","choices":[{"label":"Approach the staring socks","to":"o_gang"},{"label":"Peek into the lost-and-found box","to":"o_lostfound"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_gang","title":"The Spin Cycle","art":"sock_crowd","text":"They call themselves the Spin Cycle. The leader is an enormous hockey sock named Big Tube, stretched and scarred, with a tag that reads *machine wash hot* like a dare. Flanking him: a pair of argyles who finish each other's sentences, a toe sock with five opinions, and a tiny no-show sock nobody ever notices until it is behind you.\n\n\"Suburban,\" Big Tube rumbles, sniffing your fabric softener. \"Wool blend. Cold wash only.\" The gang snickers. \"You want help getting home? Everybody wants help. Help costs. Either you ride Number Seven, the hottest dryer in the joint, full cycle, or you pay tribute.\"\n\nBehind you, the lost-and-found box rustles invitingly.","choices":[{"label":"Ride Number Seven","to":"o_challenge"},{"label":"Pay tribute with Vernon","to":"h_door","requires":"has_quarter"},{"label":"Retreat to the lost-and-found box","to":"o_lostfound"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_challenge","title":"Number Seven","art":"laundromat","text":"Number Seven is a beast. When the door swings shut behind you, the drum is so big you cannot see the far side. Then it starts. Heat like August. Tumbling like a mountain falling over and over. A stranger's towel slaps you. Someone's bathrobe tie wraps around your heel. You hold on to the one thing you have, which is the thought of Pim, calm and folded, waiting.\n\nForty-five minutes later, the door opens. You flop out, frizzled, smelling of someone else's lavender, and very slightly shrunk. The Spin Cycle is staring.\n\n\"Full cycle,\" whispers an argyle. \"Wool blend. On *high*.\" Big Tube lowers his cuff, which is the most respect a hockey sock can show.","choices":[{"label":"Ask them to get you home","to":"h_door"},{"label":"Go see what's in the lost-and-found","to":"o_lostfound"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_lostfound","title":"Lost and Found","art":"laundromat","text":"The box is warm and smells of cardboard and patience. Inside, dozens of single socks lie together, waiting for owners who will never come back. Unlike the Hall of the Unpaired, nobody here has given up. They have simply decided to be content in the meantime.\n\nA sock rolls over to meet you. A right sock, charcoal gray with a pink toe and a heel worn thin from long walks. \"Hi,\" she says shyly. \"I'm a right. You're a left. I couldn't help noticing.\" She has a small darn on her toe too, the same shape as yours.\n\nAbove the box, the attendant taps a sign: UNCLAIMED ITEMS DONATED FRIDAY. Today is Thursday.","choices":[{"label":"Stay with the gray sock","to":"o_adopted"},{"label":"Thank her, and head back out","to":"o_street"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"o_adopted","title":"Found in the Lost and Found","art":"mismatch","text":"On Friday, the attendant does not donate the box. Instead she tips it out on the folding table and starts matching socks the way some people do crosswords, humming. She picks you up, then the gray sock with the pink toe. She holds you side by side. She laughs and says, \"Close enough,\" and balls you together.\n\nYou go home with her that night, to a different drawer in a different house, cozy and cedar-scented. Your new pair tells you about the walks she wore her heel thin on. You tell her about the dryer, the long way here, and Pim.\n\nYou never match. Everyone who opens the drawer says so. Neither of you minds at all.","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"Found in the Lost and Found","kind":"good"}},
{"id":"x_portal","title":"A Soft Fold","art":"portal","text":"Something gives way beneath you, the way a pillowcase gives when you finally find its opening. There is no fall. Only a long, slow *fwump*, like being folded by very patient hands, and then you are lying on grass the color of a baby blanket. Behind you, the fold still hangs open, breathing warm, dizzy air. You are alone. Folds deliver travelers one at a time, like a hand unloading a dryer.\n\nThe sky is lavender. Clouds tumble past, all one way, humming the low hum of a dryer on Delicate. A clothes peg signpost reads WELCOME, ODD ONES, and beneath it: *Everyone ends up here eventually.*\n\nYou check yourself for damage, then check your memories, which is scarier. *Pim. Right foot. Lilac. Calm as a folded towel. Survived a dog. Came home with me in a three-pack, one paper band round the middle.* All there.\n\nYou say it again, to be sure. It is a little quieter the second time.","choices":[{"label":"Follow the pastel path","to":"x_meadow"},{"label":"Knock at the little cozy cottage","to":"x_cozy"},{"label":"Wriggle back through, wherever it leads","to":"start"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"x_meadow","title":"The Pastel Plains","art":"odd_world","text":"The path is soft as a bath mat and winds through fields of dandelion fluff. Everywhere you look, socks are living their lives. None of them are with other socks.\n\nA crew sock jogs past arm in arm with a shoelace. A knee-high naps in a hammock strung between two clothes pegs, curled around a sleeping oven mitt. Nobody seems to mind. Everybody seems, honestly, delighted.\n\nA sundial in the middle of the meadow has no numbers, only little drawings of a dryer drum, each one turned a bit further. A sign beneath it says: *It is currently Cycle 4,112. Lunch is at Cycle 4,113.*\n\nThree roads lead on. One smells of warm bread. One jingles. One climbs toward a tall, tall shape on the horizon that seems to be watching you.","choices":[{"label":"Follow the smell of bread","to":"x_bakery"},{"label":"Follow the jingling","to":"x_market"},{"label":"Head toward the tall shape","to":"x_matchmaker"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"x_cozy","title":"The Cottage of Mr. and Mrs. Argyle","art":"tea_cozy","text":"The cottage turns out to be a tea cozy, quilted and enormous, with a round door cut in its side. The door opens before you knock.\n\n\"A visitor!\" cries an argyle sock in a tiny apron. Behind her, the cottage itself rumbles warmly. \"Darling, we have a visitor!\" \"So I feel,\" says the tea cozy, from everywhere at once. You realize, with a little lurch, that the argyle sock is *married to the house*.\n\n\"We met three hundred cycles ago,\" Mrs. Argyle says, beaming, ushering you onto a cushion. \"I was so lonely when I arrived. Now look at me. Never cold a day since.\" She pours something steaming into a thimble. \"You look like you've come a long way, Stripes. Sit. Have some tea. Forget your troubles.\"\n\nOn the mantel, in a bottle cap frame, is a picture of an argyle sock exactly like her. \"Who's that?\" you ask. She peers at it fondly. \"Do you know, I've never been sure.\"","choices":[{"label":"Sit down for tea","to":"x_tea"},{"label":"Thank them and head back out","to":"x_meadow"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"x_tea","title":"Tea at the Cozy","art":"tea_cozy","text":"The tea tastes of fabric softener and summer afternoons. It goes straight to your toe. Mrs. Argyle tells you about the time she lost a button that wasn't hers, and the tea cozy chuckles so warmly the walls fog up.\n\n\"And who are you looking for, dear?\" she asks. \"My pair,\" you say. \"My pair is called...\" You stop. The name is right there, like a sock under the bed you can see but can't reach. It starts with a soft sound. P? B? Something calm.\n\n\"Oh, they all say that at first,\" Mrs. Argyle says kindly, topping up your thimble. \"It passes. Everything here passes. That's the lovely thing.\"\n\nThe steam curls up between you, smelling exactly like being forgiven for something you can't remember doing.","choices":[{"label":"Have just one more cup","to":"x_fade"},{"label":"Ask the way home, quickly","to":"x_matchmaker"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"x_bakery","title":"Knit and Knead","art":"bakery","text":"The bakery is run by a gym sock and a leather glove, and they are clearly, unbearably in love. The glove kneads dough with all five fingers while the sock rolls the loaves into shape by flopping on them. The sign above the door reads KNIT & KNEAD: BREAD FOR THE UNPAIRED.\n\n\"Fresh lint loaf?\" the glove offers, holding out something golden and fuzzy. \"Made from the finest dryer fluff. We get it delivered from the Trap.\"\n\nThe sock leans over the counter. \"First time in the Dimension? You've got the look. Little bit frayed around the cuff.\" It squeezes the glove's thumb. \"Don't worry. Everybody finds somebody here. Even if it's not who they were looking for.\"\n\nFlour drifts down like the end of a tumble cycle. It is very cozy and it smells like staying.","choices":[{"label":"Ask if they've seen a lonely mitten","to":"x_mittpair","requires":"met_mitt"},{"label":"Offer to help with the baking","to":"x_oven"},{"label":"Ask who's in charge around here","to":"x_matchmaker"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"x_mittpair","title":"The Other Mitten","art":"mitten","text":"\"A mitten?\" The glove's fingers go still. \"Red, with a snowflake on the back?\"\n\nIt calls toward the back room, and out comes a small red mitten in a hairnet, dusted in flour. A snowflake on her back. A string trailing from her cuff, ending in nothing, exactly like Mitt's.\n\n\"Mitt?\" she whispers. \"I'm Mott. Last winter I blew off the sledding hill, rolled through half the town, and fell into a soft fold in a laundromat dryer. I thought Mitt had forgotten me. Everyone forgets, here.\" She presses a strand of red yarn into your cuff, unraveled from her own thumb. \"Red yarn always finds the Return Spool. If you see Mitt, in here or out there, tell it the bakery's past the sundial. Tell it I'm *sorry about the snowball*.\"\n\nYou don't know what happened with the snowball. You decide that's between mittens.","choices":[{"label":"Follow the red yarn to the Spool","to":"x_spool"},{"label":"Ask her who rules this place","to":"x_matchmaker"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"x_oven","title":"Second Batch","art":"bakery","text":"You flop on dough. You are, it turns out, excellent at flopping on dough. The glove applauds with its whole self. The gym sock teaches you to brush the tops with melted fabric softener so they shine.\n\nOne batch becomes two. Two becomes six. Outside, the sundial ticks over by a whole cycle, then another, and you barely notice. It is warm in here. You are useful. Nobody is asking you where your pair is.\n\nYou catch your reflection in a baking tray and pause. Teal and mustard. Red heel. A little darn on the toe. For a second you can't remember why the darn makes you sad, or who used to tell you it didn't matter.\n\n\"Another batch?\" the glove asks, beaming.","choices":[{"label":"Sure, one more batch","to":"x_fade"},{"label":"Step outside and follow the jingling","to":"x_market"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"x_market","title":"The Market of Spare Parts","art":"spin_clock","text":"The jingling turns out to be a market, stalls made from upturned thimbles and matchboxes. Everything for sale is a single something. One earring. One cufflink. One contact lens, very proud of itself. \"Loose buttons! Half a zipper! Get your strays!\" shouts a vendor that is a single chopstick.\n\nAbove it all looms a clock tower, its face a round porthole of glass behind which a tiny drum turns and turns. \"Don't stare at the Spin Clock,\" a passing mitten warns. \"It tells you how long you've been here.\"\n\nAt the end of the row, a pincushion with a needle for a nose sits under a sign: DARNS REMOVED. GOOD AS NEW. Her customers come away smooth-toed and smiling and a little vague, like someone who has lost their keys and decided not to mind.\n\nYour darned toe curls. Pim always said the darn didn't matter. That it meant somebody had loved you enough to mend you.","choices":[{"label":"Look up at the Spin Clock","to":"x_clock"},{"label":"Let the pincushion mend your toe","to":"x_end_new"},{"label":"Let Vernon work the crowd","to":"x_vernon","requires":"has_quarter"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"x_vernon","title":"Market Intelligence","art":"coin","text":"\"Leave this to me,\" says Vernon, rolling out of your cuff. \"Markets are my people.\" He works the stalls like a politician, spinning on his edge, trading gossip for gossip, and comes back glowing.\n\n\"Okay. So. The big lady on the horizon, Madame Purl? Asks every newcomer three questions about their pair. Get them right, she sends you home. Get them wrong...\" He jingles darkly. \"Also, there's a pair out by the lighthouse in your exact stripes.\"\n\nThen he stops. \"Stripes. Quick. What year am I?\"\n\n\"1999,\" you say. \"Delaware.\"\n\n\"I *knew* that,\" he says, too fast. \"I knew that.\" He has gone pale, for a quarter. \"I've never had to ask before. This place wears the edges off you. Whatever we're doing, let's do it before I forget I'm money.\"","choices":[{"label":"Go and face Madame Purl","to":"x_matchmaker"},{"label":"Head out to the lighthouse","to":"x_thirdpair"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"x_end_new","title":"Good as New","art":"odd_world","text":"The pincushion is very quick. A snip, a tug, a few clever stitches, and your toe is smooth and whole, as if nothing had ever worn through it. As if nothing had ever happened to you at all.\n\nYou feel wonderful. Light. New. You thank her and wander back into the market, admiring your perfect toe, and somewhere between the button stall and the chopstick you notice you have no idea where you were going. Or who you were going to.\n\nSomeone puts you on a stall. That seems right. A little card is pinned to your cuff: LEFT SOCK, STRIPED, LIKE NEW. Shoppers pick you up and put you down.\n\nSometimes, under the Spin Clock, you feel an itch on your toe where something used to be. You can never remember what. It was probably nothing. It was probably nothing at all.","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"Good as New","kind":"bad"}},
{"id":"x_clock","title":"The Spin Clock","art":"spin_clock","text":"Behind the glass, a little drum turns. Beneath it, a counter rolls over with a soft click every rotation. Every sock who arrives gets a tally mark on the tower wall, and yours is already there, freshly chalked: *Stripes. Cycle 4,112.*\n\nAn old tube sock leaning on a cane joins you. \"I count my cycles,\" it says. \"Every one. That's the trick. The ones who stop counting forget where they came from. Then they forget what they came for.\" It taps its cane on the cobbles. \"I came for a hiking boot. Can't remember its face. But I remember counting, and that's something.\"\n\nIt squints at your stripes. \"Funny. There's a pair out at the lighthouse wearing your pattern exactly. Teal and mustard. Been here ages.\"\n\nYour fibers stand on end. You start counting.","choices":[{"label":"Head out to the lighthouse","to":"x_thirdpair"},{"label":"Go find whoever runs this place","to":"x_matchmaker"}],"ending":null,"flag":"x_warned"},
{"id":"x_matchmaker","title":"Madame Purl","art":"madame_purl","text":"Nobody rules the Odd Sock Dimension. But ask anyone anything, the way home, who's in charge, whether it's lunch yet, and they all point the same way: toward the tall shape on the horizon.\n\nUp close, she is a sock puppet the size of a water tower, stitched from a thousand lost socks, argyle and athletic and fuzzy bedsocks, with dinner-plate buttons for eyes and a mouth that opens like the lid of a washer. Nobody's hand is inside her. Nobody needs to be.\n\n\"Ahhh,\" says Madame Purl, in a voice like every drawer closing at once. \"A new one. Lovely stripes. Tragic darn. I am the Matchmaker, little left sock, and nobody here stays single for long. I have a *wonderful* partner in mind for you.\"\n\nBehind her, a long queue of unlikely objects waits, each holding a tiny bouquet of lint.","choices":[{"label":"Hear the offers","to":"x_offers"},{"label":"Say you already have a pair","to":"x_quiz"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"x_offers","title":"The Candidates","art":"suitors","text":"Madame Purl presents them with a sweep of her heel.\n\nFirst, an oven mitt, scorched in one spot, who promises you will never feel cold again and that he has been to Thanksgiving four times. Second, a single leather driving glove with a mysterious past, who says nothing at all but winks with its middle knuckle. Third, a tea cozy, round and quilted and patient, who simply says, \"I'd keep you warm. I'd keep you so warm.\"\n\n\"The cozy,\" Madame Purl murmurs. \"I've had my eye on the two of you for cycles. You'd be very happy, dear. Very, very happy. Happier than you've ever been in that cramped dark drawer.\"\n\nThe tea cozy rustles shyly. Somewhere far away, so faint you might be imagining it, you think you feel a calm right sock waiting.","choices":[{"label":"Say yes to the tea cozy","to":"x_wedding"},{"label":"Decline. You already have a pair.","to":"x_quiz"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"x_quiz","title":"Prove It","art":"quiz","text":"\"Everyone has a pair when they arrive,\" Madame Purl says, not unkindly. \"Then the cycles turn and the pair goes blurry, like a photo left in a pocket through the wash.\" Her button eyes catch the lavender light. \"Three questions. Answer true and I'll show you the way home. Answer wrong, and well. Then you're already one of us.\"\n\nThe queue of suitors leans in. The tea cozy looks hopeful. Far off, a clock with a tiny drum for a face clicks over.\n\n\"First,\" says Madame Purl. \"What is your pair *like*?\"\n\nYou reach for the memory. It is thinner than it was when you landed, like a sock worn at the heel, and three answers rise at once, each one feeling a little bit true.","choices":[{"label":"Brave. Pim is scared of nothing.","to":"x_fade"},{"label":"Calm. Steady as a folded towel.","to":"x_quiz2"},{"label":"Anxious and fidgety, just like me","to":"x_fade"}],"ending":null,"flag":"x_quizzed"},
{"id":"x_quiz2","title":"The Second Question","art":"quiz","text":"Madame Purl's mouth twitches at the corner, which for a puppet her size is a seismic event. \"Calm. Yes. I can see the shape of that in you. You lean on calm things.\"\n\nThe name comes back sharper: *Pim.* It lands like a dropped clothes peg, small and certain. Pim lying flat in the drawer while you fretted. Pim saying, \"We always turn up.\"\n\n\"Second question,\" says Madame Purl. \"Every sock who has been through something has a story. What did your pair survive?\"\n\nThe tea cozy sighs and sits back down. The memory flickers as you grab for it: teeth or claws? Something with a wet nose, or something that purred?","choices":[{"label":"A cat. Pim survived a cat.","to":"x_fade"},{"label":"A lawnmower, somehow","to":"x_fade"},{"label":"A dog. Pim survived a dog.","to":"x_quiz3"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"x_quiz3","title":"The Last Question","art":"quiz","text":"\"A dog,\" Madame Purl repeats, and the whole queue of suitors gasps, because in the Odd Sock Dimension that is the stuff of legend. \"Chewed, carried, buried under a cushion, and came out calm. Remarkable.\"\n\nYou glow a little on Pim's behalf. Pim never brags about it. You brag about it for both of you.\n\n\"Last one, little left sock, and it's the one nobody gets.\" The Matchmaker lowers her enormous head until her button eyes are level with your toe. \"How many of you were there? When you were new, with the paper band still round your middles. How many came home from the shop together?\"\n\nSomething old and quiet stirs in your weave. A thing nobody talks about.","choices":[{"label":"Three pairs. We came in a three-pack.","to":"x_remember"},{"label":"Two. Just Pim and me, always.","to":"x_fade"},{"label":"Six pairs, in a fancy gift box","to":"x_fade"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"x_fade","title":"Fading","art":"fading","text":"Something slips. It is the feeling of a stitch coming loose, very quietly, somewhere you can't see.\n\nYou try to think of your pair and get only a soft blur in a drawer, a vague warmth. Calm? Was it calm? There was a word for it. A short word, like a pin. Like a pin? The pastel light is so comfortable. The hum of the clouds is so steady. Whoever it was, they surely wouldn't want you to fret.\n\n\"There, there,\" someone says, and something soft and warm settles over you like a hug.\n\nYou look down at the darn on your toe. You have the oddest feeling it used to mean something.","choices":[{"label":"Let the name go","to":"x_wedding"},{"label":"Cling to your darn and spin away","to":"start"},{"label":"Count the cycles, like the old sock said","to":"x_count","requires":["x_warned","x_quizzed"],"unless":"x_counted"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"x_count","title":"Counting","art":"odd_world","flag":"x_counted","text":"You count. *Cycle 4,112.* It is like pulling a loose stitch tight. *4,113.* The blur gets an edge. *4,114.* A drawer. A lilac toe. A calm voice saying, \"We always turn up.\"\n\nYou keep going, out loud now, the way the old tube sock taught you, and every number is a stitch the Dimension cannot quite pick out.\n\nA shadow falls over you, tall as a water tower. Madame Purl has been watching the whole time.\n\n\"Counting,\" she says. \"That old tube sock has been talking.\" Her button eyes narrow, not unkindly. \"Very well. Once more, from the top. But only once, little left sock. Nobody gets three tries at their own heart.\"","choices":[{"label":"Face her questions again","to":"x_quiz"},{"label":"Spin away while you still remember","to":"start"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"x_remember","title":"Remembered","art":"return_spool","text":"\"A three-pack,\" you say, louder than you meant. \"One paper band. A striped pair, a lilac pair, and us: one of each, the pair that made no sense. The striped pair went missing our first winter. Nobody talks about it.\"\n\nThe silence is enormous. Then Madame Purl laughs, a sound like a whole load of towels falling out of a basket, and the suitors applaud politely and drift off home.\n\n\"Oh, you're a stubborn one,\" she says. \"Good. Stubborn ones get home.\" She points one enormous heel at the horizon, where a great wooden spool turns slowly against the lavender. \"The Return Spool. Every thread home is wound on it.\" Her other heel drifts, as if by accident, toward a lighthouse blinking at the far edge of the meadow.\n\nThen she looks at you a moment longer than she needs to. Her stitches, up close, are very old and very tired.","choices":[{"label":"Take the Return Spool home","to":"x_spool"},{"label":"Follow her other heel to the lighthouse","to":"x_thirdpair"},{"label":"Ask why she seems so tired","to":"x_tired"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"x_spool","title":"The Return Spool","art":"return_spool","text":"Every road home in the Dimension ends at the Return Spool. Up close it is a wooden spool taller than a laundry basket, humming like a dryer winding down, and its threads run out across the lavender sky into soft folds, each one quivering toward somewhere real.\n\nOne thread is warm and smells of fresh laundry: it tugs toward a basket of clean clothes. One is pulled tight and straight toward the foot of a tall wooden shape with drawers, and high at the top of it you sense, very faintly, something calm and patient. One is frayed and tangled and spins in lazy circles, and following it would mean starting over somewhere dark and warm and dizzy.\n\nYou grip the thread you choose with your whole cuff. The Dimension starts to fold itself up around you like a fitted sheet done properly, for once.","choices":[{"label":"Follow the warm thread to the basket","to":"h_basket"},{"label":"Follow the tight thread to the dresser","to":"h_drawer"},{"label":"Grab the frayed thread and spin","to":"start"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"x_thirdpair","title":"The Lighthouse","art":"lighthouse","text":"At the edge of the Dimension, where the pastel grass gives way to a sea of fluffy white, stands a lighthouse made from a rolled-up bath mat. Its light is a single bright lint ball, glowing.\n\nTwo socks are on the gallery, polishing the lamp. Teal and mustard stripes. Red heel patches. No darns at all. They look exactly like you, twice.\n\n\"Stripes?\" says one. \"*Little* Stripes? From the three-pack?\" The striped pair: the one nobody talks about. They slipped through a fold together the first winter and never came back. \"We built the lighthouse,\" the other says, \"so anyone from our drawer who ended up here would see it and find us.\" They look you over. \"You took your time. And you're alone. Where's Pim? Where's the lilac one?\"\n\nYour heart, which is mostly wool, does something complicated.","choices":[{"label":"Stay at the lighthouse with them","to":"x_end_three"},{"label":"Ask them to come home with you","to":"x_beacon"},{"label":"Ask them the way home","to":"x_spool"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"x_beacon","title":"Keeping the Light","art":"odd_world","text":"\"Come home with me,\" you say. \"Both of you. The drawer has room.\"\n\nThe striped pair look at each other the way matched socks do, all at once. \"The drawer,\" says one, slowly. \"Was it the top one? Was it warm?\" The other frowns. \"There was a smell. Wood, maybe.\" They remember you perfectly. They remember Pim. They have kept the lamp lit so long that they have forgotten everything else, even the way back.\n\n\"Somebody has to keep the light on,\" the first says at last, gently. \"For the next one of us who falls through.\" She pinches a spark of glowing lint from the lamp and tucks it into your cuff. \"You go. Tell Pim we said the pair that made no sense was always the best one.\"\n\nThe spark is warm against your heel. The lamp turns its slow white circle.","choices":[{"label":"Carry the spark to the Return Spool","to":"x_spool"},{"label":"Stay and help keep the lamp lit","to":"x_end_three"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"x_tired","title":"The Matchmaker's Secret","art":"madame_purl","text":"Madame Purl lets out a long breath that ruffles the whole meadow. \"Tired? Little left sock, I have been matching for ten thousand cycles. Every lonely sock who falls through, I find them someone. I make them happy.\" Her button eyes droop. \"I never once found anyone for me. You can't match the matchmaker. Those are the rules.\"\n\nShe looks at you, at your stubborn stripes, your darn you've stopped hiding. \"You remembered when nearly nobody does. That's what the job needs. Someone who knows what a pair is *for*.\" She holds out a knitting needle, long and silver and humming. \"Stay. Take my needle. Let me finally rest. Or go home, and I'll keep stitching. Either way, I won't hold it against you.\"\n\nThe needle is warm. Pim is somewhere far away.","choices":[{"label":"Take the needle","to":"x_end_matchmaker"},{"label":"Gently refuse and go home","to":"x_spool"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"x_wedding","title":"Happily Cozied","art":"wedding","text":"The wedding is held at Cycle 4,200 in the Pastel Plains. The gym sock and the glove do the cake: a three-tier lint sponge with fabric-softener icing. The Argyles are front row, crying. Your groom is a tea cozy, round and quilted and patient, the one Madame Purl, the Matchmaker, always had her eye on for you. She officiates, booming over the meadow: \"Do you, Stripes, take this tea cozy?\"\n\nYou do. You honestly, gladly do. The cozy wraps around you and you are warmer than you have ever been, warmer than any drawer.\n\nSometimes, late in a cycle, you wake up with a word on the tip of your toe. Something short. Something calm. You lie there trying to remember who it belonged to, and then your tea cozy rustles in its sleep and pulls you closer, and the word drifts off like lint on a breeze, and you are happy. You are, strangely, completely happy.","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"Happily Cozied","kind":"weird"}},
{"id":"x_end_three","title":"Three of a Kind","art":"lighthouse","text":"You stay. It isn't forgetting, not this time. You talk about Pim every evening on the lighthouse gallery, and the others tell you everything: how they slipped through, how they waited, how they kept the lamp lit for their drawer.\n\nEvery cycle, the three of you send a ribbon of light out across the fluffy sea, into every soft fold in reality, aimed at one particular dresser in one particular house. A beacon in teal and mustard. *We're here. We remember. Come when you're ready.*\n\nIt isn't the drawer. It isn't Pim. But it is family, the oldest kind, the kind that came home from the shop in the same paper band. And on clear cycles, when the beam reaches farthest, you swear you feel a calm little tug from very far away, as if someone on the other end is holding the light and not letting go.","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"Three of a Kind","kind":"good"}},
{"id":"x_end_matchmaker","title":"The New Matchmaker","art":"needle","text":"You take the needle. Madame Purl sighs, and it sounds like a whole drawer settling, and then her stitches loosen one by one and a thousand old socks drift gently off across the meadow, free, each to its own wrong-but-happy home.\n\nThe needle teaches you everything. You learn to see the threads between things: the oven mitt and the dish towel, the lonely sock and the shy pot holder. You make them happy. You are very, very good at it.\n\nEvery new arrival, you ask the same thing first: \"Tell me about your pair.\" And you listen, and you help them remember, and when they do, you send them home.\n\nSometimes, late in a cycle, you knit a single teal and mustard stripe and tuck it into the Return Spool, pointed at a dresser far away. You never forget Pim. That was the whole point.","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"The New Matchmaker","kind":"bittersweet"}},
{"id":"w_rinse","title":"Rewash","art":"washer","text":"A giant hand closes around you and lifts you into the light. Far above, the Owner sniffs you, once, and makes the face people make at milk.\n\nYou know what they smell. Dog, maybe. Street, maybe. Or the sour little ghost that creeps into any load left damp too long. It doesn't matter. The verdict is the same. \"Nope,\" says the Owner, and you fly.\n\nYou land in a white steel cavern full of strangers: a towel, three gym socks, a red sweatshirt so new it still has its tag, and a pair of swim trunks announcing to the whole load that he is Captain Trunks. The round glass door slams. A porthole, you realize. A dial clicks to *Normal*. Somewhere a valve opens with a long, hungry hiss.\n\nYour tag says *cold wash only*. Nobody has read it. This is not a room. It is a sea, and it is coming in.","choices":[{"label":"Grab the nearest thing that floats","to":"w_captain"},{"label":"Sink to the bottom and brace","to":"w_fill"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"w_fill","title":"The Flood","art":"washer","text":"The water pours in from the back of the drum like a waterfall that has been waiting all week for this. It is cold. Nobody read your tag; the Owner is just cheap. You will take it. You are soaked from heel to cuff in a heartbeat, and your darn stings with the shock.\n\nThe flood lifts the towel like an iceberg. Then something rolls out from under it, where the Owner tossed it before anything else: a plump, glossy capsule, blue and green and swirling, glowing like a tiny planet.\n\nIt bobs beside you. Its skin is already thinning. \"Oh good,\" it says, in a voice like bubbles. \"A listener. Quick, I haven't got long.\"\n\nOverhead, Captain Trunks drifts past, legs puffed with air, shouting orders at nobody.","choices":[{"label":"Listen to the glowing pod","to":"w_oracle"},{"label":"Swim for the swim trunks","to":"w_captain"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"w_captain","title":"Captain Trunks","art":"captain_trunks","text":"You catch hold of a drawstring and haul yourself aboard. The swim trunks float like a raft, both legs swollen with trapped air, a pattern of tiny palm trees across the deck.\n\n\"Welcome aboard the *Board Short*,\" booms the captain. \"Captain Trunks. I have crossed this sea three hundred times. I have seen chlorine. I have seen the *ocean*, the real one, with sand in my pockets for a month.\"\n\nHe looks you over. \"Single, eh? Shame. I've never had that problem. Two legs, one garment. I *am* my own pair.\" He says it kindly, which somehow makes it worse.\n\nBelow, three gym socks paddle in a ring, eyeing you with piratical interest. Above, a glowing pod is dissolving as it mutters to itself.","choices":[{"label":"Sign on with the gym sock crew","to":"w_crew"},{"label":"Paddle over to the glowing pod","to":"w_oracle"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"w_oracle","title":"The Pod Speaks","art":"pod","text":"The detergent pod turns slowly in the water, shedding blue swirls with every word. \"I am the Pod,\" it says. \"I know all things. I have about forty seconds. Listen.\n\n\"Beware the red one, for it bleeds and does not know it. Beware the blue song in the last rinse, for it is soft and wants you softer. Beware the hose, for it goes down and never up. The ghost will help you, if you are not afraid of the pale. And the door is a shore. All loads come to shore.\"\n\nIt is getting very small now. \"Also, you have a darn on your toe. It's fine. Nobody notices. That one was free.\"\n\nThen it is gone, and the whole sea smells of *Mountain Spring*. The drum gives a lurch. Then another. The weather is turning.","choices":[{"label":"Hold on tight","to":"w_storm"},{"label":"Swim for the three gym socks","to":"w_crew"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"w_crew","title":"The Odd Lot","art":"odd_lot","text":"The three gym socks are a crew. They call themselves the Odd Lot, and each lost a pair in some heroic way. Whiff lost hers in a locker room. Crusty lost his to a dog park, and wears an iron-on knee patch over one eye to prove it. The smallest, a no-show called Ghostie, isn't sure he ever had one.\n\n\"Sign the articles,\" says Whiff, holding out a soggy receipt. \"Code of the Odd Lot: no sock left behind, no sock left matched, and all treasure split three ways.\"\n\n\"Four ways now,\" says Crusty, glaring.\n\nYou press your heel to the receipt. It dissolves at once, which everyone agrees counts. Whiff grins. \"First job, new sock: there's treasure in the deep. Something sharp and shiny fell out of a jeans pocket last load.\" Under you, the drum gives a lurch. Weather is coming.","choices":[{"label":"Dive for the treasure","to":"w_pick"},{"label":"Ride out the storm with the crew","to":"w_storm"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"w_pick","title":"The Guitar Pick","art":"washer","text":"You dive. The water is cloudy with suds, and down at the bottom, wedged between two holes in the drum, something glints: a guitar pick, tortoiseshell, worn smooth on one edge from years of the same three chords.\n\n\"Don't,\" it says as you touch it. \"I'm retired. The Owner loses one of us a week. We turn up in sheets, under dryers, in the dog. I have seen things in the bottom of a tip jar that would curl your elastic.\"\n\nYou explain that you are trying to get home. The pick considers this for a long, plasticky moment.\n\n\"Fine. One last gig,\" it says, and lets you tuck it into your cuff. \"I'm good at two things. Strumming, and wedging into places nobody can reach. Let's hope you need one of them.\"","choices":[{"label":"Rejoin the crew before the storm","to":"w_storm"},{"label":"Explore the rubber door seal","to":"w_gasket"}],"ending":null,"flag":"w_has_pick"},
{"id":"w_storm","title":"Agitate","art":"captain_trunks","text":"The storm hits.\n\nThe drum heaves left, stops dead, heaves right. Waves of soapy water slam over everything. Captain Trunks roars into the spray, \"HOLD FAST, YOU BEAUTIFUL LAUNDRY!\" The towel goes overboard, then comes back, then goes overboard again. The three gym socks cling together in a soggy knot, singing a shanty that is mostly just yelling.\n\nYou lose your grip. The sea flings you across the drum like a wet comma. Lightning would be dramatic here, but the washer settles for a loud clunk every few seconds, which is honestly worse.\n\nWhen you surface, gasping suds, you see three things: a pale, ragged shape drifting toward you through the foam, a great red hulk wallowing in a spreading pink cloud, and the rubber seal of the door, wrinkled like a reef.","choices":[{"label":"Swim toward the pale shape","to":"w_ghost"},{"label":"Swim toward the red hulk","to":"w_red"},{"label":"Make for the rubber reef","to":"w_gasket"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"w_ghost","title":"The Bleached","art":"ghost_shirt","text":"The pale shape is a T-shirt. Or it was. It was black once, a concert shirt, but now it is splotched with bleach in the shape of continents, so thin you can see the drum through it. It drifts toward you, sleeves out, moaning. \"Cooooold. Waaaash. Ooooonly.\"\n\n\"That's on my tag too,\" you say. The ghost stops moaning and peers closer. \"Teal and mustard,\" it whispers. \"I know you. The bleach pen, two years ago. You were in that load, you and a lilac one, clinging together the whole cycle like you'd been sewn. The pen missed you both. It found me.\" It does not sound bitter. \"I'm Tour Dates. Go home. Somebody should.\n\n\"Listen to the dead: the red one does not know what it does. And the rubber reef by the door is safe.\" Pink is spreading through the water like a rumor.","choices":[{"label":"Head for the rubber reef","to":"w_gasket"},{"label":"Go and warn the red one","to":"w_red"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"w_red","title":"The Red Sweatshirt","art":"red_sweatshirt","text":"Up close, the red sweatshirt is enormous and very, very cheerful. It says STATE across its chest in white letters that are going faintly rosy. Its tag, still attached, flaps like a little flag.\n\n\"HI!\" it bellows. \"First wash! Isn't this AMAZING? Everyone's so NICE!\" Around it, the water glows a deep, lovely pink. A white undershirt nearby has gone the color of a strawberry milkshake and looks stunned.\n\n\"You're bleeding dye,\" you say, carefully. \"Am I?\" The sweatshirt peers down at itself. \"Huh. Is that bad? Do you want a hug? I give great hugs. I have a hood.\"\n\nYou feel the warm pink current tugging at your stripes. Teal and mustard. You have always been teal and mustard. You have never wondered what else you could be. Until now.","choices":[{"label":"Accept the hug","to":"w_pinkend"},{"label":"Paddle away fast and wait out the wash","to":"w_calm"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"w_pinkend","title":"Pink Forever","art":"pink_forever","text":"The hug is tremendous. The sweatshirt wraps you in its hood and sleeves and pure good intentions, and the pink soaks into you like sunlight. Your teal goes rose. Your mustard goes coral. Even your red heel patch blushes deeper, as if to say, finally.\n\nWhen the lid opens, the Owner stares into a load of nothing but pink, says a word socks are not supposed to hear, and gives the whole lot to a small niece who receives it like a coronation.\n\nShe wears you to school, to the park, to bed. She wears you with a different pink sock every day and calls you both \"the twins.\" You are rose and coral and loved, and every time you pass a lilac thing you think of Pim, and think: we were always going to end up in the same family of colors.","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"Pink Forever","kind":"weird"}},
{"id":"w_gasket","title":"The Rubber Reef","art":"gasket","text":"You hook your toe into the door seal and hold on. The gasket is a reef of folds and grooves, and every fold holds a secret: a hair tie, a wad of tissue that went through the wash in a pocket and has become a kind of seafloor snow, and three pennies who have lived here so long they have started a small religion.\n\n\"Welcome, pilgrim,\" say the pennies in unison. \"Rest. The storm passes. The storm always passes. Then comes the calm. Then comes the turning. Then the shore.\"\n\nYou rest. Outside the reef, the sea thrashes, and then, between one clunk and the next, it stops. The water drains away and floods in fresh and clean. You realize the pennies are right, and a little smug about it.","choices":[{"label":"Swim out into the calm","to":"w_calm"},{"label":"Stay on the reef for the turning","to":"w_spin"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"w_calm","title":"The Rinse","art":"washer","text":"The rinse is the calmest sea you have ever known. Fresh water, no soap, no storm. The drum rocks gently, the way a cradle would if cradles were made of steel. Captain Trunks floats on his back, legs up, humming. The gym socks doze in a heap. Even the red sweatshirt has gone quiet, staring at its pinking letters in mild confusion.\n\nYou drift. For the first time since the buzzer, you are not scared. You are clean. Your stripes feel brighter. The darn on your toe feels less like a flaw and more like a story.\n\n\"Enjoy it,\" the captain calls. \"Rinse never lasts. Next comes the turning, and the turning separates the sailors from the lint.\"\n\nHigh in the machine, something small clicks open. A faint, silky humming begins.","choices":[{"label":"Ask Vernon what he knows","to":"w_vernon","requires":"has_quarter"},{"label":"Brace for the turning","to":"w_spin"},{"label":"Float closer to the humming","to":"w_softener"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"w_vernon","title":"Vernon at Sea","art":"coin","text":"\"Psst,\" says Vernon. The quarter has been riding in your cuff the whole time, Delaware side up, 1999 and proud of it. \"I've been through this washer four times. I know the score.\"\n\n\"You never said.\"\n\n\"You never asked. Listen. When it spins, everything flies to the wall. The small stuff goes out the holes and down the hose. I went down once. Fished out by the Owner's plumber. Twelve dollars an hour to save a twenty-five-cent coin. Worst day of my life. Also best, financially speaking.\"\n\nHe settles deeper in your cuff. \"Grab something big and hold on. Something with legs. And whatever you do, do not let go when the wall starts singing.\"","choices":[{"label":"Find the captain and hold on","to":"w_spin"},{"label":"Float on and enjoy the calm","to":"w_softener"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"w_spin","title":"The Turning","art":"spin_wall","text":"It starts slow. The drum turns, then turns faster, then faster than turning seems allowed. The sea pulls away from the middle and climbs the walls in a roaring silver whirlpool, and you go with it, flattened against steel full of little holes.\n\nThe wall sings. It is a high, terrible note. Water streams out through the holes beneath you, tugging at your weave, pulling, pulling. A shoelace goes. A button goes, with a tiny scream.\n\nNearby, Captain Trunks is pinned spread-eagle to the drum, magnificent and unbothered. \"GRAB A LEG, STRIPES,\" he bellows over the howl. His drawstring whips past you. Below you, through the holes, you can feel the dark pull of something long and hollow, sighing.","choices":[{"label":"Grab the captain's leg","to":"w_unload"},{"label":"Let the water take you","to":"w_hose"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"w_hose","title":"The Drain Hose","art":"drain","text":"You slip through the gap where the drum meets the tub and into the dark. Then the river takes you.\n\nThe drain hose is long and ribbed and gray, and it smells of old soap and older sorrows. The water moves slowly here, mournful. Things drift past you: a fleck of tissue, a thread, a single sequin, glinting once and gone. Somewhere ahead, the hose bends down toward the wall and the pipes and the great beyond.\n\nA voice echoes along the ribs, low and patient. \"All things come down the hose eventually,\" it says. It is the hose itself. \"Lint. Thread. Hope. Do not struggle. The current is kind, in its way.\"\n\nYou can feel it pulling you toward the bend. But behind you, faintly, the washer is still sloshing and gulping overhead, not finished with its sea.","choices":[{"label":"Wedge in the pick, climb to the captain","to":"w_unload","requires":"w_has_pick"},{"label":"Swim back against the current","to":"w_backwash"},{"label":"Let the river carry you","to":"w_drainend"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"w_drainend","title":"Down the Drain Hose","art":"drain_hose","text":"You stop fighting. The river carries you to the bend, where the hose meets the wall, and there you catch. Your heel snags on a ridge, and your cuff folds over, and you stick fast, a woolen plug in a plastic throat.\n\nAt first you are frightened. Then the next load drains, and you notice: the sequins and threads and hair ties catch in your weave instead of sinking into the pipes. You are a filter. The hose hums with gratitude. \"No one has ever stayed,\" it says.\n\nYou stay. Load after load, you hear the washer above: storms and calms and the singing wall. You catch what falls. You keep the pipes clear. Sometimes you think of Pim, folded in the cedar dark, and you hope Pim never needs a rewash. And sometimes, between loads, the hose tells you stories, and they are better than you expected.","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"Down the Drain Hose","kind":"bittersweet"}},
{"id":"w_backwash","title":"Upstream","art":"drain","text":"You swim. Socks are not built to swim, but you are a left sock, and left socks are stubborn. You inchworm up the ribs of the hose against the current, one ridge at a time, while the hose sighs, \"Nobody ever goes back up.\"\n\nThen, far above, the washer kicks into one more rinse, and the water in the hose reverses with a great gulp. A surge of backwash catches you and flings you up the hose, through the gap, and back into the drum, where you land in a heap on top of something warm, red, and delighted.\n\n\"HI!\" shouts the red sweatshirt. \"WHERE DID YOU GO? I MISSED YOU! I miss everybody! Hug?\"\n\nAcross the drum, Captain Trunks is waving his drawstring. \"Stripes! The last spin's coming! Get over here!\"","choices":[{"label":"Get to the captain","to":"w_unload"},{"label":"Fine. One hug.","to":"w_pinkend"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"w_unload","title":"Shore","art":"washer","text":"The wall stops singing. The drum winds down, slower, slower, and settles with a click. You are plastered to the steel beside Captain Trunks, wrung out, clean, and alive. The gym socks cheer weakly. The red sweatshirt is pinker than anyone expected, including itself.\n\nThen the porthole swings open and light pours in. The Owner's hands scoop out the whole heavy, tangled load, you included.\n\n\"Shore,\" sighs the captain. \"Fair winds, Stripes.\"\n\nA thumb finds your stripes and pauses. Wool. The hand hovers between the round steel door you know far too well and the basket of warm, dry laundry waiting by the wall. You have a strange feeling you have been here before. You have a strange feeling you will wake to a buzzer.","choices":[{"label":"Get tossed in the basket of dry laundry","to":"h_basket"},{"label":"Tumble into the dryer, again","to":"start"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"w_softener","title":"The Blue Song","art":"washer","text":"The click came from a little drawer high in the machine, one you never noticed. Now it tips open, and the softener comes down.\n\nIt pours into the sea in a long silky ribbon of blue, and it is singing. Not words, exactly. More the feeling of a towel straight off the line. *Let go,* it sings. *You are so soft now. Nobody has to hold on to anything. Drift down, down, where the water goes.*\n\nYour grip loosens. All around you, laundry goes limp and dreamy. The towel is smiling at nothing. Even Captain Trunks has stopped shouting, his drawstring trailing, his eyes half closed.\n\nFar below, the little holes in the drum are humming the same tune, and the drain is listening.","choices":[{"label":"Clamp your cuff shut and hold on","to":"w_spin"},{"label":"Ask the pick for one last song","to":"w_gig","requires":"w_has_pick"},{"label":"Follow the song down","to":"w_hose"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"w_gig","title":"The Last Gig","art":"captain_trunks","text":"The guitar pick climbs out of your cuff and squints at the blue song like a heckler. \"Softener,\" it mutters. \"I've played rooms like this. Everybody mellow, nobody listening.\"\n\nThen it braces its worn edge against Captain Trunks's drawstring and strums.\n\nThree chords. The same three it has played for years. They twang off the steel and come back doubled, and the sea starts to slosh in time. The gym socks wake and stamp. The towel hums the bass line. The captain opens one eye, then bellows the chorus of a shanty nobody taught him, and the whole load roars it back.\n\nThe blue song falters, sulks, and slides off down the drain alone.\n\n\"Still got it,\" says the pick, tucking itself back into your cuff. Under you, the wall begins to turn.","choices":[{"label":"Grab the captain's leg and hold on","to":"w_unload"},{"label":"Ride the turning on your own","to":"w_spin"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"j_drawer","title":"The Junk Drawer","art":"junk_drawer","text":"A giant hand scoops you up along with whatever else was lying around, carries the lot to the kitchen with the air of someone thinking about something else entirely, and drops you into a drawer. The drawer slams. A hundred small things settle into new and interesting disappointments.\n\nIt is dark, and it smells of rubber, old pennies, and soy sauce. Something pokes your heel patch. Something else is ticking, though nothing in here has told the right time since daylight saving.\n\n\"Welcome,\" says a thin, wiry voice near your toe. \"Don't panic. Nothing in here is lost. It is just not found yet.\"\n\nAround you, the dark mutters. Batteries grumble in a corner. Far off, something long and thin clicks against the wood, alone, like it is waiting for someone who is not coming.","choices":[{"label":"Ask the wiry voice where you are","to":"j_twist"},{"label":"Approach the grumbling batteries","to":"j_batteries"},{"label":"Follow the lonely clicking","to":"j_chopstick"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"j_twist","title":"The Twist-Tie","art":"junk_drawer","text":"The voice belongs to a twist-tie, paper-wrapped and bent into a thoughtful question mark. It once held a loaf of bread closed for nine days and has been thinking about it ever since.\n\n\"Everything in the house that is not a sock ends up here,\" it says. \"Batteries. Birthday candles. A key that opens nothing. You are our first sock. Either you are very lost, or the drawer is broadening its definitions.\"\n\nIt twists itself a little tighter, the way philosophers do. \"You heard the rule. Nothing in here is lost. It is just not found yet. Lost is a feeling. Not found yet is a *schedule*.\" It pauses. \"A long schedule. Some of us are on year eleven.\"\n\nYou think of Pim, waiting in the cedar dark, and wonder how long a schedule a sock can stand.","choices":[{"label":"Tour the drawer's economy","to":"j_market"},{"label":"Ask about the key that opens nothing","to":"j_key"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"j_batteries","title":"The Council of Double-As","art":"junk_drawer","text":"In the corner sits the Council: six AA batteries, two AAAs, and a nine-volt who chairs every meeting because nobody dares touch its terminals. All of them are, by any reasonable test, dead.\n\n\"We are *not* dead,\" says the eldest AA, whose label is peeling. \"We have a little left. Put us in a remote and see. Enough for one more channel.\"\n\n\"One more channel,\" the others murmur, like a prayer.\n\nThey have ruled the drawer since the Owner moved in, because they are the only ones who have ever been inside a machine that mattered. The nine-volt regards you with a faint, dangerous tingle. \"A sock. Unusual. State your business, and keep your lint away from our ends.\"","choices":[{"label":"Petition the Council to let you leave","to":"j_vote"},{"label":"Back away toward the middle of the drawer","to":"j_market"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"j_market","title":"The Packet Exchange","art":"junk_drawer","text":"The middle of the drawer is a marketplace. The currency is soy sauce packets. Duck sauce trades at half value, and there is one packet of hot mustard so precious that nobody can afford to break it. Rubber bands do all the heavy lifting, stretched across the gaps as bridges, and they are talking openly about a strike.\n\nThe bank is run by the birthday candles, a solemn order of half-melted wax who each burned for forty seconds once and have never stopped talking about it. \"We were *wished upon*,\" says a pink one with a striped twist. \"Have you ever been wished upon?\" You admit you have mostly been stepped on.\n\nBetween the stalls, a little brass key goes from object to object, trying itself in each one and apologizing. And from under everything, a folded paper voice calls out: \"Ordering? We're open! We are definitely open!\"","choices":[{"label":"Answer the paper voice","to":"j_menu"},{"label":"Follow the apologizing key","to":"j_key"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"j_menu","title":"Happy Garden","art":"junk_drawer","text":"The voice is a takeout menu. HAPPY GARDEN, it says across the top, FREE DELIVERY OVER $15, beside a drawing of a smiling dumpling. The restaurant closed in 2014. Nobody has told the menu, and nobody is going to.\n\n\"Number twenty-three is very popular,\" it says. \"General Tso's. Extra crispy. Can I take your order?\"\n\nYou order nothing, gently. The menu doesn't mind. It flips over to show you a delivery map on the back, every street in faded red ink, this very house circled in pen because the Owner used to order every Friday.\n\n\"If you need to get somewhere,\" it says, \"ask the tape measure. It goes everywhere the Owner goes. And our chopstick is still here. We always sent two. Lost one in 2013. It never really recovered.\"","choices":[{"label":"Find the tape measure","to":"j_tape"},{"label":"Go and meet the chopstick","to":"j_chopstick"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"j_key","title":"The Key to Nothing","art":"junk_key","text":"The key is small and brass, with a hole in its head where a ring once went. It has been in the drawer for six years and it opens nothing. It has been told this. It does not *know* it.\n\n\"Are you a lock?\" it asks, with terrible hope. \"You have an opening at the top. Just let me try.\" You explain that you are a sock and the opening is for a foot. The key tries anyway, very politely, then sits down on a birthday candle.\n\n\"Somewhere there's a door that misses me,\" it says. \"A shed. A diary. A bicycle. I would settle for a bicycle.\" It gazes at the thin dark gap at the very back of the drawer, where things slip down and never come back. \"Some nights I think the lock I belong to is back there.\"","choices":[{"label":"Slip into the dark gap with the key","to":"x_portal"},{"label":"Look for a safer way out","to":"j_tape"},{"label":"Visit the lonely chopstick","to":"j_chopstick"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"j_chopstick","title":"The Other Odd One","art":"junk_chopstick","text":"At the far end of the drawer, propped against a corkscrew, lies a single wooden chopstick. Its paper sleeve is gone. Its pair is gone too, and you can tell, because it is lying exactly the way you have been lying since the dryer stopped.\n\n\"Happy Garden, 2013,\" it says before you can ask. \"We came in one sleeve, joined at the top, like we'd been born together. Then a dinner party. Then just me.\"\n\nYou tell it about Pim. Lilac, calm, survived a dog. The chopstick listens to the whole thing, which almost nobody does.\n\n\"You're long, and thin at the ankle, and missing someone,\" it says at last. \"So am I. I'm not saying anything. I'm just saying we would fit very neatly in a drawer.\"","choices":[{"label":"Become a pair of odd ones","to":"j_end_takeout"},{"label":"Look for a way out of the drawer","to":"j_tape"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"j_vote","title":"Motion to Be Found","art":"junk_drawer","text":"The Council convenes on a coaster. The whole drawer crowds in to watch: rubber bands, candles, soy sauce packets, and a thumbtack who is only here for the drama.\n\n\"The sock,\" announces the nine-volt, \"moves to be *found*.\" A gasp ripples across the packets. Nobody has asked that in years. The last to try was a tiny screwdriver, taken out to fix the Owner's glasses and never returned. Some say it went to a better place. Some say the garage.\n\nThe batteries filibuster for an hour about how much charge they have left. When the vote finally comes, the motion fails nine to eight, the twist-tie abstaining on philosophical grounds.\n\n\"You may stay as a citizen,\" the nine-volt rules, sparking faintly. \"Remember. Nothing in here is lost.\" In the back, the tape measure clears its throat, like someone with a dissenting opinion.","choices":[{"label":"Accept the ruling and settle in","to":"j_end_notfound"},{"label":"Appeal to the tape measure","to":"j_tape"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"j_tape","title":"Seventy-Two Inches","art":"junk_drawer","text":"The tape measure is the only thing in the drawer with a job. It sits in its yellow case like a retired sea captain, and it has been everywhere: the hallway, the top of the stairs, the bedroom, even the inside of the dryer, the day the Owner wondered about a bigger one.\n\n\"Seventy-two inches of opportunity,\" it says, and lets out a little tongue of tape to show you. \"Here's the plan. This drawer opens for scissors or for me. Scissors go out the front door to open packages. I go upstairs to measure the bedroom, every time. Whichever comes out, you hold on.\"\n\nIt reels the tape back in with a deeply satisfying *zip*. Overhead, giant footsteps cross the kitchen, and stop.","choices":[{"label":"Wait for the drawer to open","to":"j_open"},{"label":"Visit the lonely chopstick first","to":"j_chopstick"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"j_open","title":"Where Are the Scissors","art":"junk_drawer","text":"Light floods in. The drawer shudders open and the entire economy slides toward the front: packets, candles, the Council clinging to one another. A giant hand plunges in, and from far above comes the oldest prayer in any kitchen: \"*Where* are the scissors.\"\n\nThe hand gropes past the menu, past the key, and closes on the scissors. Then it comes back, patting around, and finds the tape measure too. \"Scissors for the parcel on the wet back step,\" the voice mutters. \"Tape for that dresser upstairs.\" For half a second both are right beside you: the scissors' plastic loop, and the tape measure's little metal hook, held out to you like a hand.\n\nBehind you, the twist-tie calls softly. \"No shame in staying. Not found yet is still a kind of home.\"","choices":[{"label":"Ride the scissors out to the back step","to":"h_door"},{"label":"Ride the tape measure up to the dresser","to":"h_drawer"},{"label":"Stay. Someone has to wait here.","to":"j_end_notfound"}],"ending":null},
{"id":"j_end_notfound","title":"Not Lost, Just Not Found","art":"junk_drawer","text":"You stay. The dark settles around you like a blanket that smells faintly of soy sauce.\n\nYou become a citizen. You hold the hot mustard packet in trust for the market. You take a seat on the Council, the first member with no terminals at all, and you are well liked, because you are soft and you cushion the AAs when the drawer slams. Every year the birthday candles ask what you would wish for. You always give the same answer.\n\nSometimes, through the wood, you hear the dryer buzz down the hall, and you think of Pim, folded in cedar, lilac and patient.\n\nNew arrivals come in scared: pen caps, orphaned screws, a lonely earbud. You tell them the rule, kindly, and you almost believe it. Nothing in here is lost. You are just not found yet.","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"Not Lost, Just Not Found","kind":"bittersweet"}},
{"id":"j_end_takeout","title":"Takeout for Two","art":"junk_chopstick","text":"You lie down beside the chopstick. It is not Pim. It is not even fabric. But it fits along your length the way a good idea fits a sentence, and when the drawer slams you roll together and do not come apart.\n\nThe drawer is scandalized for about a week, which is a long time in a drawer. The twist-tie writes a treatise. The batteries refuse to recognize the union. Then the Owner, rummaging for a pen, pulls you out together, stares, and laughs so hard the whole drawer rattles.\n\nNow you are a duster. One sock, one chopstick, a rubber band holding you snug. Every Sunday you go under the refrigerator together, into a darkness no hand can reach, and come back gray and triumphant with lost hair ties, to the cat's fury.\n\nYou are an odd pair. You are, unmistakably, a pair.","choices":[],"ending":{"name":"Takeout for Two","kind":"weird"}}
];
